\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 4rd UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Launches Y1 Tln  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Yen Tumbles\b0\par\par\uc2 1,206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 13:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds ministry comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Wednesday for the first time in more than six years, launching a wave of yen selling totaling an estimated Y1 trillion to counter what it calls "the severe economic conditions" facing the country.\par \par \uc2 The initial barrage of yen-selling orders hit the market early in the Asian day after the dollar fell to a 15-year low of Y82.87.\par \par \uc2 It was apparently the last straw for authorities who had fretted for weeks that the strengthening yen was posing severe risks to the economy and especially its export-driven corporate titans.\par \par \uc2 The dollar shot higher, and the initial wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was followed by bursts that pushed the greenback above Y85. At 1145 GMT the dollar was at Y85.28 after reaching a high of Y85.53, a gain of nearly Y3 from its low for the day.\par \par \uc2 The massive selling began with an initial wave of Y200 billion-Y300 billion, followed by continual bursts to total an estimated Y1 trillion through the Asian trading day, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The authorities then continued selling yen into European trading and were prepared to extend the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the U.S. session, a Japanese government official said. The official said the Finance Ministry's action was partly aimed at countering speculative yen buying.\par \par \uc2 If the Finance Ministry were to let up, the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 "I'd give the yen about a 60% chance of staying strong," said Hideki Amikura, deputy general manager at Nomura Trust and Banking, predicting that there would be substantial yen buying by exporters repatriating overseas earnings whenever the dollar nears the Y85.00 level.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  about 20 minutes after the first sales hit the market.\par \par \uc2 "We conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier to curb excessive fluctuations in exchange rates," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda told a hastily arranged news conference.\par \par \uc2 "Since yesterday, the yen has further strengthened. In our country's economy, deflation is continuing, and we are in severe economic conditions," he said, adding that Japan acted on its own, but was in close contact with foreign governments.\par \par \uc2 Japan has been threatening for days to take "decisive action," including possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official hinted that Wednesday's action was not the end.\par \par \uc2 "When \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes rates, there will be new buyers and sellers and (we) respond to that continuously," said the official, who spoke on condition he not be identified. Another official said the authorities aren't targeting any specific yen level.\par \par \uc2 If history is any guide, the markets may be in for a long slog. When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to push the yen down, it sold Y35 trillion from January 2003 to March 2004. One university study estimated the spending at around Y285 billion each time the government weighed in.\par \par \uc2 Corporate leaders who had been pushing the government to act as they saw profits from their critical overseas markets shrink, applauded the news. \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . said it hopes the government will continue to take appropriate steps to stop the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 The Japan Chamber of Commerce went further, with Chairman Tadashi Okamura saying that had the government not waited so long, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been more effective." He also said that it would be "appropriate" for the dollar to rise to Y95.\par \par \uc2 Stocks surged, with the Nikkei Stock Average posting its biggest gain in over five weeks led by major exporters such as \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Sony\uc2 . The index closed up 2.3% at 9516.56.\par \par \uc2 "It's positive as a first move. The timing came as a surprise for players who had been betting on the yen to rise and stocks to fall" after Prime Minister Naoto Kan was re-elected president of the Democratic Party of Japan, said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said in a statement he "strongly expects" the action "will contribute to a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rate formation." He also said that the BOJ will continue to maintain "strong monetary easing."\par \par \uc2 Augmenting that impact, the BOJ will leave the extra yen from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, rather than mopping up the excess liquidity by "sterilizing" the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people familiar with the matter told Dow Jones. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees "no need to absorb" the extra yen as that could blunt the effectiveness of the Japanese government's efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 This could be an important measure to increase Japan's monetary base as a further weapon to fight the deflation that has long dragged on Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 This comes less than three weeks after the BOJ called an emergency meeting Aug. 30 to pump more liquidity into the economy.\par \par \uc2 The longer-term success of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains in doubt, however, and will be decided as much by overseas developments, especially in the U.S. economy, as by Japan's actions.\par \par \uc2 "The yen is rising on the back of global moves to keep home \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak and amid continuing expectations for further monetary easing in the U.S.," said Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. "Unless there are fundamental changes in these areas, it will not be possible to stem the yen's rise," he added.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said that effective \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would require coordination with other countries, notably the U.S. With its own drive to increase exports, Washington is not expected to show much enthusiasm towards Japan's steps to make its own exporters more competitive, especially as the U.S. pressures China to let the yuan rise faster.\par \par \uc2 Others, however, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a useful move designed to break the dollar's steady slide since June.\par \par \uc2 "This is aimed at warning people off and breaking the one-way bet that Japan has been experiencing," said Peter Kenen, a professor of economics at Princeton University and a former Treasury consultant under several administrations. He called the move "both feasible and sensible."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the dollar against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The South Korean won, Taiwan dollar, Singapore dollar and Chinese yuan all fell on the news. Analysts said that central banks in the region, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  often in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, now may have a stronger argument to weaken their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and boost the competitiveness of their own export sectors.\par \par \uc2 There was also the question of whether Japan was focusing on the right issue at all.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Stock Exchange President Atsushi Saito welcomed the move but said that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely "symptomatic therapy" for the root issue of deflation, a view echoed by \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like putting a Band-Aid on a person, in this case a country bleeding from deflation," he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is not a fundamental solution."\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 07:46 ET (11:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f002j0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
