\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Bank Of Moscow Bonds Slip With Mayor's Standing\b0\par\par\uc2 1,436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 14:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Moscow (MMBM.RS) bonds fall after state media portray Mayor Yuri Luzhkov in a negative light, sparking speculation the Kremlin may oust him. The price of the bank's bonds due 2105 fell markedly, pushing the yield to 6.18%, VTB Capital says. "It would appear that the slew of negative reports, both on TV and in print, about the activities of Moscow's Mayor Yuri Luzhkov were the main reason for this," analyst Maxim Korovin explains. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD was little changed after July manufacturing sales declined more than expected, notching a 0.9% slip. The market had expected a 0.2% increase. USD was at 1.0290, from 1.0283 ahead of the data. It was at 1.0243 late Tues, according to EBS via CQG. karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1235 GMT [Dow Jones] Within the August 0.6% rise in total import prices, fuel prices gained 1.7%. The rise was led by a 2.1% increase in petroleum prices -- the largest advance since April -- which more than offset a 3.5% decline in natural gas prices. Excluding petroleum, import prices were 0.2% higher in August. Food import prices were up 2.2% in August, the largest one-month increase since March 2008. Rising prices for vegetables, meat and food oils all contributed to the increase. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. import prices rose more than expected in August, thanks to higher energy and food prices, a move that should help quell fears that the economy may fall into deflation. The price of goods imported into the U.S. rose 0.6% last month, following a 0.1% rise in July, revised from a previously reported 0.2% increase. Economists were expecting import prices to increase 0.3% in August.\par \par \uc2 (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] New York manufacturing activity is expanding this month, but at a slower pace than in August, according to the New York Fed's Empire State Manufacturing Survey. The business conditions index fell to 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August, worse than 7.0 expected reading. The employment index remained positive, but little changed, standing at 14.93 in September from 14.29. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits 85.67, up about 3% from 83.08 late Tue, on back of first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in six years. Market awaits next move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "MOF likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in NY," to boost its chances of eventual success, said Steven Englander, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citi. After \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian and European trading, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  prepared to take action again in the US market if needed, a Japanese government official said Wed. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1225 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD at 1.2978, down from 1.3020 late Tue, ahead of Empire State manufacturing index due at 8:30am ET. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the talk of market ahead of the domestic data marker. USD/JPY at 85.52, from 83.08, according to EBS via CQG. GBP/USD at 1.5540, from 1.5563. Consensus for Sep index at 5.0, after 7.1 result for Aug. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1222 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys fall for the first time this week as many investors booked profit following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. The move fuels strong rally in the Japanese stock market, prompting shift from Treasurys into stocks. The sharp fall in the yen versus the dollar also sparked hedging-related selling in Treasurys as many traders hedge bets on a complex financial instrument whose value linked with the dollar/yen, overshadowing MOF's buying of the dollar, whose proceeds are usually parked in Treasurys. Up next is Empire State manufacturing report and import prices data at 8:30 a.m. ET. The Fed will buy Treasurys at 10:30 a.m. ET, targeting maturities between September 30, 2014 and August 31, 2016. 10-year note's price is 11/32 lower to yield 2.721%. The 30-year bond is 1 2/32 lower to yield 3.861%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [Dow Jones] Homeowners who could refinance have already started the process, leading the MBA refi index to fall 10.8% this week. The 35 BP rise in Treasury rates over the last month didn't help, although mortgage rates did not fluctuate that much since homeowners were never offered rates at rock bottom levels. 30-yr rates at 4.47%, origination fees continue to kick up (now 1.08 pts) and capacity has probably been reached. Purchase index was down 0.4%. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1208 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: The BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Wednesday for the first time since March 2006 reportedly spending as much as Y1Tln to halt USD/JPY's slide below Y83.00. The spot immediately added around one yen from its fresh 15-year low of Y82.87 and climbed to a high of 85.54 in Europe while EUR/JPY peaked at a one-month high of 111.11. Dips in USD/JPY were limited to around 85.00 after an MOF official let it be known that further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had taken place during the European session. So far the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been unsterilized, which means it increases money supply and is effectively a form of monetary easing. The BOJ's surprise move helped the dollar recovery elsewhere after Tuesday's sharp sell-off triggered by speculation that the Fed will ease monetary policy again before year end. EUR/USD slipped from 1.3024 to 1.2955, USD/CHF and EUR/CHF added one cent to 1.0049 and 1.3048, respectively, amid speculation that the BOJ's action could trigger a response from the SNB ahead of Thursday rate set meeting. GBP/USD took a small hit after the UK August claimant count unexpectedly rose by 2.3k instead of falling by 4k. However, late session had fully recovered to print the day's high of 1.5569. Elsewhere, gold traded $2 under Tuesday's fresh all-time high of $1274.75oz and the main European bourses are between 0.2% and 0.4% lower. European government bonds are lower with some traders citing the BOJ's unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a factor. December bunds are currently down 0.25 at 130.26 while December gilts are 0.32 lower at 122.90 not helped by a lackluster auction of 20-year gilts. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY at 85.43, from 83.08 late Tue, on back of first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in six years, selling about 1 trillion JPY during Asian and European hours to hinder the yen's appreciation. Investors will be watching closely for any signs of Japan's MOF once US trading become active. Japan prepared to take action again in the US market if needed, a Japanese government official said Wed. EUR/JPY climbs to 110.58 from 108.20, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD slightly off at 1.2987 from 1.3020 late Tue, while GBP/USD at 1.5550 from 1.5563. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1155 GMT [Dow Jones] While some of Treasury strength Tues. was attributed to reports there would be another announcement of Fed buying, Richmond Fed Pres Lacker tells WSJ the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only do more if it was looking at a real threat of consumer price deflation, something he doesn't see. "For me if the growth numbers come in about where the consensus forecast is and we continue to get inflation between 1% and 2%, I don't believe I would see a need for further stimulus. Others may disagree but that's how I see it," Lacker said. Treasurys weak early, despite short end support after yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .(darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2010/09/14/richmond-feds-lacker-wants-high-threshold-for-more-fed-action/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+wsj%2Feconomics%2Ffeed+%28WSJ.com%3A+Real+Time+Economics+Blog%29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2010/09/14/richmond-feds-lacker-wants-high-threshold-for-more-fed-action/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+wsj%2Feconomics%2Ffeed+%28WSJ.com%3A+Real+Time+Economics+Blog%29 }}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 15, 2010 08:36 ET (12:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f002vq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
