\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:Japan's Yen Move Is Quick Fix That Ignores Deeper Problems\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yuka Hayashi  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,067 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 16:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday was an attempt to apply short-term emergency treatment to one symptom of a shaky economy--a rising yen--while avoiding measures to address the underlying causes.\par \par \uc2 One reason policy makers resorted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite widespread doubt about its effectiveness: They have largely exhausted their options for more-powerful cures. After nearly two decades of trying to prop up the economy with extensive fiscal and monetary steps, Japan is grappling with government debts nearly the twice the size of its economy, and interest rates hovering at or near zero since the mid-1990s. That severely limits what officials can do.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's trillion-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -dumping \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was immediately effective in pushing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down sharply against the dollar and the euro, and then pushing up Japanese stocks. But it did nothing to address what many economists consider to be the key drivers of the yen's recent rise. One is the fact that American and European central banks have more room to lower rates than their Tokyo counterparts, boosting the relative value of yen-denominated assets. Another is Japan's persistent deflation, which also makes the yen--and yen-based investments--grow in value, compared with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Behind those problems, Japan has for years dragged its feet on implementing more-painful steps to fix structural problems, such as a hefty dependence on profits generated from exports. Lingering regulation has left domestic sectors, such as finance and retail, ineffective and weak. And women's participation in the workplace remains low, even as a shrinking work force caused by an aging population puts a tight cap on economic growth.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Stock Exchange President Atsushi Saito called the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  merely "symptomatic therapy."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro said, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like putting a Band-Aid on a person--in this case a country bleeding from deflation....This [\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] is not a fundamental solution."\par \par \uc2 Any short-term relief provided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move could even delay further reforms. "We are worried that by providing temporary relief from the pain of a strong yen, [the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] will again allow \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to postpone steps needed to address fundamental problems it must solve," said Hiroshi Shiraishi, an economist for \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came the day after Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan won an internal party-leadership election to defend his job as national leader and signaled he was ready to put his focus back on fighting economic problems following weeks of brutal infighting within the ruling party that kept him and his top deputies distracted. He scored a decisive win against a rival lawmaker who pledged more-aggressive economic steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kan said after his victory Tuesday: "The challenge that needs to be tackled the most immediately and forcefully is to rebuild the economy and establish job safety."\par \par \uc2 Kan said his government now will focus on implementing policies stipulated in the government's economic growth strategy unveiled earlier this year. The package includes promoting infrastructure exports to emerging economies and helping companies engaged in green technology. He said these steps are aimed at "escaping 20 years of deflation, building a strong economy and creating jobs."\par \par \uc2 The yen's steady rise in recent months has played a role in damping what had been a moderately optimistic outlook for the Japanese economy. A higher yen threatens to undermine business confidence by shrinking yen-based revenue for Japanese exporters while deepening deflation by lowering the prices of imports. It also can undermine employment by causing manufacturers to shift production overseas.\par \par \uc2 The annualized rate of growth in the Japanese economy slowed sharply to 1.5% in the April-June quarter, compared with 5% in the first quarter and 3.4% in the last quarter of 2009. Economists say a higher yen, and slowing momentum in overseas markets, could limit Japan's growth further in coming quarters.\par \par \uc2 With growth prospects weakening, politicians have been stepping up pressure on the Bank of Japan to ease its monetary policy, even though its monetary policy has already been extremely easy. That prompted the BOJ to unveil a new step to increase its short-term lending late last month, but it has had little discernible effect on the market. Lawmakers are now calling for more drastic steps, including requiring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adhere to an inflation target. BOJ leaders say there is little more they can do and that politicians need to rely more on fiscal policy to lift growth.\par \par \uc2 Kan last week announced a new economic-stimulus package that included such steps as employment assistance for new graduates and encouraging foreigners to invest in Japan. But at $11 billion, the package is a far cry from past stimulus packages, and economists doubt its immediate effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 The price tag was kept low to avoid boosting Japan's outsized borrowing, a move that could spook bond markets, trigger ratings agencies to mark down Tokyo's sovereign debt, and push up interest rates, further damping economic activity.\par \par \uc2 In that environment, yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be the easiest quick fix, at least removing one factor aggravating the economy. But many economists and policy makers also view government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an ineffectual tool in manipulating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, citing the huge size and complex nature of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa wrote in his 2008 book, before he joined the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : "When the countries concerned make a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on an unified judgment about the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, there are cases in which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective. In other words, it's hard in general to expect a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be effective unless those conditions are met."\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Tokyo was in close contact with financial authorities from other major nations, but that it acted alone in Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "In the past, a significant strong yen trend was reversed by coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Osamu Takashima, a \uc2 Citigroup\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist in Tokyo. "Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had little such effect."\par \par \uc2 -By Yuka Hayashi, The Wall Street Journal; 81 36 2692 832; yuka.hayashi@wsj.com [ 09-15-10 1034ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100915e69f0009w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
