\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ MONUMENT SECURITIES: BoJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Lewis  \par \uc2 1,200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 16:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2    Of Monument Securities \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (BoJ) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier today came after the yen had strengthened further in response to news of Mr Kan's victory in the contest for leadership of his party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 After a month or more during which Japanese policymakers had talked of their disquiet at the yen's continuous appreciation, accompanying their comments with not-so-veiled threats that they were ruling out no measures to cap the yen's rise, their credibility was on the line.\par \par \uc2 They could plausibly argue that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not have been very effective before the result of the DPJ vote was known. Market participants would have been aware they might need to reassess prospects after the result was declared and would, therefore, have had scant confidence in the stability of any exchange rate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivered.\par \par \uc2 But, even after the leadership election, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be very effective. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have been co-ordinated with other central banks, it presumably would have been. After all, co-ordinated moves make so much more impression on market players than solo forays.\par \par \uc2 It seems extremely unlikely, therefore, that other central banks will be backing up the BoJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BoJ action underscores Japanese authorities' isolation in agonising over a strong yen. On the other hand, the Kan Government and the BoJ may have removed one of the factors that had been pushing the yen higher, namely, the market's interest to establish at what level the BoJ's yen-selling would occur. Further, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain unsterilised, if only to forestall US objections that the Japanese authorities are manipulating their exchange rate. The resulting net increase in yen deposits should exert minor downward pressure on the yen.\par \par \uc2 Even so, global capital flows will continue to dominate yen trading. Japan's current account surplus together with foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  diversification into yen assets represent two yen-positive fundamentals skewing the odds against a weaker trend developing in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It would probably take a significant revival of the 'yen carry trade' to bring about a substantial fall in the yen exchange rate. That, in turn, would depend on two conditions being fulfilled. First, Japanese interest rates would have to remain at ultra-low levels; this is very likely. Secondly, there would have to be a fall in global investors' risk-aversion well beyond what has been seen so far this year even in the markets' 'risk-on' phases; this seems less likely to occur.\par \par \uc2 Much will hang on Japanese institutions' investment policies after the financial half-year-end on 30 September. With the spreads of Japanese bond yields below their counterparts in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sectors having narrowed, Japanese institutions' buying of foreign bonds could be less in the second half of the 2010/11 financial year than it was in the first. Figures from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  show Japanese net investment in foreign bonds so far in the first half (up to 4 September) has amounted to almost Y15.7trn, compared with only Y7.9trn net buying in the corresponding period of 2009. This will have been a major factor countering yen strength and, incidentally, boosting bond prices in markets outside Japan.\par \par \uc2 The focus in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has been on the yen in recent days. But now that the Japanese authorities have settled the questions when and at what levels the BoJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , market participants may well move on in search of new themes.\par \par \uc2 They may restore the US dollar/euro relationship to a central position in their concerns. The surprise in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in recent weeks is that the US dollar has not weakened more than it has against the euro. After all, the markets have been anxious that the US economy is entering the second downswing in a 'double-dip' recession, while the talk in Europe, after impressive German output data, has been of sustained growth despite troubles in the periphery of the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the US dollar has given back less than half the gains it scored against the euro during the sovereign debt crisis earlier this year. Indeed, in terms of the Federal Reserve's broad effective rate index, perhaps a better measure of the dollar's strength in an international economy characterised by multipolar growth, the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been remarkably steady this year, trading between a 'low' of 100.21 on 11 January and a 'high' of 106.62 on 25 May. The latest calculation, for 10 September, was 102.52.\par \par \uc2 The figures reflect China's weight in US trade and the relative stability of the USA's exchange rate with China and other important Asian trading partners. They argue against assuming a large impact on the US domestic economy from movements in the US dollar/euro exchange rate, though effects on relative competitiveness in third markets should not be ignored. However that may be, the boost to US net trade and GDP from the US dollar's depreciation from 2005 to 2008, when the broad effective rate fell from an average of 112.4 in November 2005 to 95.4 in July 2008, is probably exhausted by now.\par \par \uc2 The US trade deficit has widened significantly over the past few quarters. US imports of goods and services exceeded exports by $698.8bn in 2008 but the deficit shrank to 'only' $374.9bn in 2009. In the first seven months of this year, the deficit was already standing at $288.8bn, or $495bn at an annualised rate.\par \par \uc2 This is a worrying trend, seeing that the US trade deficit has long been the most obvious sign, if not the cause, of the global imbalances problem. The other indicator of global strain has been China's trade surplus. In recent months, this, too, appears to have been growing. In the three months to February, it amounted to $40.2bn, then dipped to $14.0bn in the three months to May before soaring to $68.7bn in the three months to August.\par \par \uc2 A widening of global imbalances will contribute to dollar-negative sentiment on foreign exchanges, though this may well be punctuated by periods of alarm over euro zone stability. The yen, by contrast, remains free of such concerns.\par \par \uc2 -By Stephen Lewis: (+44) 20 7190 7193; sjlewis@monumentsecurities.com\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)\par \par \uc2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 10:05 ET (14:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f003k3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
