\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS: Japan Runs Risk Of International Criticism\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 16:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Japan faces the risk of stark criticism from major global political powers after it defied international convention with its super-charged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven richest economies, of which Japan is a member, have generally agreed in recent years that direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be used only to counter alarmingly rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climbs, particularly those stemming from speculative attacks. The yen's recent climb doesn't fit that bill.\par \par \uc2 That means that if the Bank of Japan keeps on selling yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf, U.S. officials and others may start issuing carefully worded criticisms on the Japanese move, making tense international relations on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets even frostier.\par \par \uc2 "The MOF can only afford to drive the yen so far over a series of days before criticism will likely start to emerge, particularly given the heightening tensions that have emerged on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy front over the past few weeks," said Simon Derrick, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London.\par \par \uc2 The scale of Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken most market watchers by surprise. Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sting involved as much as Y1 trillion ($12 billion) in yen sales, making this one of the authorities' biggest dollar-buying splurges on record. The scale of the slide in the yen has also been unusually large, with the dollar shooting some 3% higher to hit a peak of Y85.72 against the yen. Government officials have signaled clearly that further bouts of yen selling lie ahead.\par \par \uc2 Some market watchers believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even on this scale, will dodge international criticism, particularly given the relentless surge in the yen over recent months--a shift that has taken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a series of 15-year highs against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the Japanese economy looking shaky and exports crucial to its revival, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has looked to many like the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's only option, even despite persistent pressure on China to let the yuan rise and fall in line with market forces.\par \par \uc2 "Buying the yen had become a one-way bet," said Paul Robson, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  in London. Moreover, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has snapped its links with the forces that usually determine its direction, such as the gap between the paths markets expect interest rates to follow in the U.S. and Japan.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb has gone beyond what interest-rate spreads suggest and become a momentum-driven overshoot," Robson said. "This \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is justified," he added, regardless of analysis suggesting that in real terms, the yen isn't as strong as it may appear.\par \par \uc2 Still, in the finely worded world of diplomatic tit-for-tat on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, it is clear that major economies would like, as a whole, to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be directed by market forces. G7 statements encouraging China to allow greater yuan flexibility bear testimony to that.\par \par \uc2 In its most recent direct comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in October 2009, the G7 nations bemoaned "excess volatility" and "disorderly movements" in exchange rates. The unspoken rule is that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength in itself should be allowed to run its course. Beggar-they-neighbor devaluations aren't in the spirit of international cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Last week, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner ducked press questions over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and instead called on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to focus on securing an economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, at this point, U.S. authorities appear sanguine about the latest developments in the yen. So far, they haven't commented on the move. "An effort to weaken the yen is likely to draw far less focus than the perceived unfair trade provided by the level of the Chinese exchange rate," said a group of analysts at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , who believe that investors may overestimate the risk of a diplomatic war of words over this issue.\par \par \uc2 "It is early yet, but if U.S. authorities simply continue to decline to comment, this is implicitly turning a blind eye to the Japanese action and could serve to disappoint those looking for stronger opposition," they added.\par \par \uc2 Still, already, some financial-market heavy hitters have lashed out at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's go-it-alone strategy. After \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program started Wednesday, Jean-Claude Juncker, who chairs the group of finance ministers from the 16 countries that share the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , called \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy "inappropriate."\par \par \uc2 Derrick at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon said that further negative reactions would be "embarrassing," and that they could "undo the work that [the Japanese authorities have] already done."\par \par \uc2 At 1300 GMT, the dollar was charting a fresh high for the day, and indeed its highest level since late August, at over Y85.70.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Clare Connaghan in London contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 10:05 ET (14:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f003k5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
