\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ MONEY TALKS: Japan Does Its Bit To Stir Global Balances Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 19:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The world has changed greatly since 2004, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 As such, there's a lot more international politics playing out after Wednesday's 1 trillion-yen market dump by the Bank of Japan. In particular, it stirs up two hot international issues: that of global imbalances between surplus and deficit countries, and China's undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's hard to argue that Japan deserves a yen at 15-year highs against the dollar, to which it had repeatedly broken in recent days. With the rest of its economy in a two-decades-long deflationary funk, Japan is dependent on a viable export sector. On those grounds at least, the BOJ's actions seem reasonable.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that, unlike 2004, the U.S. is now in a sickly way as well.\par \par \uc2 Exactly two years after the \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  collapse, the aftermath of the financial crisis is still weighing heavily on the U.S. economy. Unemployment is refusing to drop and American consumers are hunkering down as housing prices slump. And yet with a trade deficit widening this year, the U.S. also needs to boost exports.\par \par \uc2 Here's the dilemma: As the world's main reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar is still the conduit through which Japan and other countries give themselves a competitive shot in the arm. That would be fine if the exchange rate were merely a global benchmark, but in reality it affects the lives of 300 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 America's once-profligate consumers are only learning to deal with the "new frugality" imposed by their debt burdens and they cannot afford to pick up the tab for everyone else. Yet a stronger dollar will inevitably steer what limited spending they make toward other country's products, hurting domestic job prospects.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that the euro is essentially unchanged on the day against its U.S. counterpart. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, while (so far) respecting the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stern message not to buy yen, has also not interpreted it as a reason to buy the dollar against everything else.\par \par \uc2 But a much bigger question hangs over China, which has doubled its share of world exports since 2004 to now account for a tenth of the total. Over that time it has deliberately kept its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak against the dollar, most often with a fully fixed rate.\par \par \uc2 That posture has increasingly angered other countries. But over the past three days, China has been receptive to diplomatic pressure, letting the yuan reach its highest level against the dollar since it began trading in 1994. This followed a visit to Beijing by Lawrence Summers, President Obama's chief economic adviser, during which the yuan policy was high on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 But the key measure will be to see what the People's Bank of China does Thursday.\par \par \uc2 When it fixed its lower dollar-yuan parity rate on Wednesday, it did so just before the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now it will likely come to market with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's second-biggest trading partner 3% weaker on the day. On that basis, if the PBOC allows another increase in the yuan against the dollar, it will cement a much larger gain in trade-weighted terms.\par \par \uc2 If it doesn't do that, China could send a negative signal to other countries. After all, the yuan is only up 1.5% against the dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime was officially made more flexible in June. It is still heavily undervalued by as much as 40%, according to some measures.\par \par \uc2 Whatever happens, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner will likely bite his tongue publicly. But behind closed doors, he and other members of the administration could well be cursing Japan, China or both.\par \par \uc2 (Michael Casey, a special writer with Dow Jones Newswires, writes a regular column about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income markets. Previously, he was Newswires' Buenos Aires bureau chief and, before that, assistant managing editor for the U.S. economy, Treasurys and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  group in New York. He can be reached at 212-416-2209 or michael.j.casey@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 13:38 ET (17:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f004lo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
