\par\par \b \uc2 BOND REPORT: Short-term Treasurys Rise On  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Deborah Levine  \par \uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 20:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Short-term Treasury prices rose on Wednesday, pushing short-term yields back toward record lows, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe that, similar to the period after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2004, the cash generated by its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make its way back into shorter-dated U.S. government debt, including bills.\par \par \uc2 Yields on 2-year notes (UST2YR), which move inversely to prices, fell 2 basis points to 0.48%, after declining to 0.45% earlier. A basis point is 0.01%.\par \par \uc2 The all-time low is 0.45%.\par \par \uc2 Yields on 3-year (UST3YR) fell by 3 basis points to 0.77%.\par \par \uc2 Longer-dated securities turned lower in afternoon trading , a correction analysts called healthy after the recent rally.\par \par \uc2 Yields on 10-year notes (UST10Y) rose 4 basis points to 2.72%, after touching 2.66%.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-year bonds yields (UST30Y) increased 7 basis points to 3.88%.\par \par \uc2 "Proceeds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that find their way to the Treasury market will most likely favor shorter-dated securities," said strategists at CRT Capital Group.\par \par \uc2 Still \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to have a strong or longer-term impact on U.S. yields, because the stable and low level of interest rates in both countries leaves little room to maneuver, said Gene Flood, chief executive and economist at Smith Breeden Associates.\par \par \uc2 "The effect is going to be very subdued," Flood said. "Rates are so close to the bottom that there's no give."\par \par \uc2 Longer-dated debt came under pressure after a strong two-day rally had traders adjusting positions with uncertainty about actions by U.S. and overseas central banks muddying the outlook.\par \par \uc2 Yields on 10-year notes have increased about 13 basis points in two days.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-year yields are up about 9 basis points.\par \par \uc2 "I still believe that the market wants to print the 2.87% level in 10-year notes," said RBS Securities strategist William O'Donnell. "I hear widespread chatter about buyers lurking just shy of 3.00% and markets have a funny way of stepping up to the door and knocking to see who is home.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  move was primary among the risks. Read about Japanese yen, dollar.\par \par \uc2 "We see policy risk as the greatest risk, and recent reports of the Japanese MoF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduce a new wrinkle into the equation," said George Goncalves, a bond strategist at Nomura. "We currently expect the market to take a pause after a strong two-day run and look to re-load at slightly higher yields."\par \par \uc2 Fed buyback, data\par \par \uc2 Separately, the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  purchased $3.89 billion in Treasury debt maturing from 2014 to 2016, part of officials' pledge to reinvest cash from maturing mortgage-backed securities and housing agency debt back into the bond market to support the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Dealers offered to sell the Fed $16.63 billion in debt.\par \par \uc2 RBS Securities expected the U.S. central banks to buy about $4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Bonds were mostly lower after a report said import prices rose 0.6% in August.\par \par \uc2 Also, the New York Federal Reserve Bank's Empire State Manufacturing index unexpectedly fell in early September. [ 09-15-10 1434ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020100915e69f0006n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
