\par\par \b \uc2 JGBs Up As Fiscal Fears Ease;  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Impact Limited\b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 09:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                                      At 0600 GMT   Change \par    TFX June 3-Mos Euroyen Price:       99.710      +0.020 \par    TSE Dec 10-Yr JGB Futures Price:    142.12       +0.74 \par    10-Yr 1.0% JGB No. 310 Yield:       1.040%      -0.090 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Lead Japanese government bond futures ended sharply higher Wednesday, as the victory of fiscally prudent Naoto Kan in a ruling party election helped ease oversupply concerns. The upside was somewhat limited, however, by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to sell yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 With the DPJ presidential election over and the bond market's fiscal concerns alleviated, players are willing to take advantage of the "excessive" JGB price falls that took place ahead of Tuesday's election, said Maki Shimizu, senior strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets Japan.\par \par \uc2 Kan won the fiercely contested Democratic Party of Japan leadership race by defeating political heavyweight Ichiro Ozawa, allowing Kan to continue as prime minister.\par \par \uc2 Longer-dated government bonds were sold off sharply after Ozawa--known as a fiscal expansionist--announced he would run in the election.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, lead December JGB futures closed up 0.74 at 142.12. The contract briefly touched the intraday low of 141.58 at the beginning of the afternoon session, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in more than six years, pushing the yen down and boosting Tokyo shares.\par \par \uc2 Players initially sold off JGBs on the MOF's action. But demand remains strong after Kan's win, and with no more long-term JGB auctions planned this month, said Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at \uc2 DLIBJ Asset Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the market will closely examine the effects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the 10-year cash JGB yield is expected to decline to around 1.0% in the near term, Yamazaki said. The benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield fell 9.0 basis points to 1.040%, as of 0600 GMT.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's Shimizu also expects any impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be limited on JGBs, and the overall bond market trend to remain solid in the latter half of this month. She said the MOF's move alone won't likely reverse the yen's broad upward momentum.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Other Cash Bond Yields            At 0600 GMT   Change \par    2-Year 0.1% JGB No. 296 Yield:      0.135%      -0.005 \par    5-Year 0.4% JGB No. 91 Yield:       0.300%      -0.070 \par    20-Year 1.9% JGB No. 121 Yield:     1.750%      -0.140 \par    30-Year 2.0% JGB No. 33 Yield:      1.885%      -0.130 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Megumi Fujikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com [ 09-15-10 0318ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020100915e69f0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
