\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBNZ Had Been Too Bullish, Now Too Bearish -ANZ\b0\par\par\uc2 2,287 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 00:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ have swung from being too bullish in their outlook to being too bearish and now look too conservative in their outlook for 2011 growth, says ANZ economist Khoon Goh; doesn't expect a hike now before March 2011, with 4.5% peak for OCR now likely. "We believe the RBNZ was too bullish on growth three months ago and commentators talking a 6%-plus OCR endgame, but are now coming in too bearish on 2011 prospects, and hence low on their assessment of where rates will end up." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD has likely done bulk of its drop after RBNZ statement, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Bernard Doyle; while most in market consider statement dovish, Doyle says: "We believe this is effectively a neutral stance." NZD/USD dropped 40 pips after statement to low of 0.7260, last at 0.7274. "However, we doubt there will be much follow through on this. The market may be reacting to the extent of the lowering in the 90-day bill track in the RBNZ's forecasts." RBNZ slashed its 90 day bills forecast to 3.4% in 4Q 2010 and 4.1% 4Q 2011 from 3.7% and 5.3%.(enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mixed, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.2% at 4668 after Wall Street rose despite weak economic data and weaker commodity prices. DJIA up 0.4% at 10572.73, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1125.07, Nasdaq up 0.5% at 2301.32. U.S. August industrial production rose 0.2% vs 0.3% expected. Capacity utilization rose to 74.7% vs 75.0% expected. S&P 500 Consumer Staples up 0.7%, with Kraft up 1.7% on upbeat outlook statements. S&P 500 Healthcare up 0.8%, with McKesson up 5.4% after reiterating earnings guidance. S&P 500 Financials up 0.4%, with KBW Bank index up 0.1%. S&P 500 Materials down 0.1%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 0.5%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 2.2%, Barrick Gold down 0.5%. BHP ADRs at A$39.13 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9383) vs local close at A$39.44. London listed BHP down 0.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 1.4%. S&P 500 Industrials up 0.1%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.2%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.2%. Spot gold at US$1,267.78 vs US$1,268.53. Nymex crude oil down 1.0% at US$76.02. LME copper down 0.5%, zinc down 1.4%, aluminum down 0.3%, nickel down 0.6%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3012 vs 1.2993. AUD/USD at 0.9379 vs 0.9398. S&P/ASX 200 last 4661.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to remain focus for markets; USD/JPY largely in range in most of European, U.S. session Wednesday after spiking in Asian trade as Tokyo waded into market; Japanese government official says will extend \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed; investors will be watching their resolve in keeping the pair up or if they move to drive it even higher; USD/JPY at 85.67 vs 85.63 late Wednesday in NY, EUR/JPY at 111.44 vs 111.36, EUR/USD at 1.3011 vs 1.3012. NZD/USD pressured lower, last at 0.7271 after RBNZ keeps rates on hold as widely expected early Thursday but decidedly cools its heels on projections for pace of any future tightening, citing impact of Christchurch earthquake, uncertain outlook for domestic, world growth. Asian stock markets have slightly positive lead from Wall Street's gains, despite weaker-than-expected NY Fed manufacturing survey, U.S. industrial production; Japanese exporters could extend gains on USD/JPY strength. Malaysian markets shut for holiday. On data slate: Japan tertiary industry index at 2350 GMT, Australia consumer inflationary expectations survey at 0100 GMT, RBA bulletin at 0130 GMT, India weekly WPI at 0630 GMT, Hong Kong unemployment at 0830 GMT, RBI policy review, BOJ governor Shirakawa speaks. Later there's ECB governing council meeting, UK retail sales, CBI industrial trends, EU trade balance, U.S. PPI, jobless claims, TICS data, foreign U.S. debt holdings, Fed governor Duke speaks, Treasury Secretary Geithner testifies, Philly Fed business outlook survey, BOE MPC member Posen speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ gaining confidence upcoming GST price increases won't necessarily feed through to inflation as subdued domestic demand is bigger driver of price action right now, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Bernard Doyle. Doesn't expect RBNZ to hike until 2011 when it's clear fragile domestic demand may have stabilised. "For now, we will leave our forecast for fresh hike unchanged at 1Q 2011. However, for us, like the RBNZ, this will depend on the domestic economy showing renewed momentum." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2234 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall after prices in U.S. slipped for a second consecutive session overnight in a setback from 1-month highs and as Egypt, world's top buyer, bought mostly French wheat in a tender. Traders remain on edge about tightened global supplies after a severe drought slashed output in former Soviet states and prompted Russia to ban grain exports. However, the markets lacked fresh weather threats or strong export news to spark a recovery, analysts said. Moreover, fears about tightening global supplies began to ease Tuesday, when Australia raised its forecast for wheat production and exports above traders' expectations. They had feared dryness in Western Australia could dent output. Soft red winter wheat on CBOT for December delivery sank 9.25 cents, or 1.3%, to US$7.2675/bushel. AUD remains strong, holding most recent gains, last AUD/USD last 0.9379, little different from this time yesterday. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$7.80 to settle at A$280.50/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia fell A$7.50 at A$308.50.(ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold loses 10 cents early in Asia at bid $1,268.10/oz, climbing after probing down to a low of $1,262.50, unable at this stage to make a fresh successful assault on record high of $1,275.75 posted Tuesday but continuing to hold most of that day's strong gains. Gold futures in NY pulled back yesterday as market participants sold holdings to cash in on gold's record highs and demand declined on a lack of market moving news. Gold prices have climbed since late July, lifted by worries about stumbling economic recoveries in U.S. and Europe. "Short term traders are taking some profits" in gold, said Frank Lesh, broker and analyst with FuturePath Trading in Chicago. "This doesn't deter anybody who is holding (gold) for the long term," he added. Gold futures fell earlier this year in the days after setting record highs. "Although the momentum has eased, precious metals remain well-bid," said Standard Bank analyst Walter de Wet, in a client note. LME base metals mostly fell yesterday, weighed down by weaker-than-expected U.S. data, speculation that China will step up measures to cool its property market. Metals managed to pare some of their losses on back of a rally in the euro, which rose against USD in late trading in Europe but gain wasn't enough to completely reverse fall in base metals prices. LME 3-month copper fell $35 vs Tuesday PM kerb to $7,615/metric ton, aluminum eased $7 at $2,153, zinc shed $28 at $2,145.00, nickel slipped $150 to $23,200, lead lost $7 to $2,235 but tin extended its rally, surging $505 at $23,150. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia loses 4 cents at $20.56/oz, platinum down $4 at $1,602, palladium unchanged at $554. Nymex October crude futures lose 78 cents overnight at $75.82/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2218 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD/JPY surges Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for 1st time in 6 years; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  says would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Thursday if necessary. USD/JPY at 85.63 late Wednesday in NY, vs 83.08 late Tuesday; EUR/JPY at 111.36 vs 108.20, EUR/USD at 1.3012 vs 1.3020. U.S. stocks up Wednesday on encouraging forecasts from \uc2 Kraft Foods\uc2  and McKesson boosted outlook for corporate sector; DJIA +0.4%, S&P 500 +0.4%, Nasdaq +0.5%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts demand for short-dated Treasurys while weakening long-dated securities; 10-year, 30-year maturities bore brunt of sales in choppy trade on belief short-dated notes among Japan's favored places to park its USD proceeds. 10-year note price down 11/32, yield up to 2.721%; 30-year bond down 1 7/32 to yield 3.869% while 3-year note up 1/32 to yield 0.766%, 2-year note up 1/32 to yield 0.488%. Oil futures prices lower on stronger USD/JPY, also as NY Fed manufacturing survey drops to lowest level since July 2009; Empire State Manufacturing index at 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August, well below 7.0 expected. October crude down 78 cents at $76.02/bbl. LME base metals lower on U.S. data (including weaker-than-expected industrial production at +0.2% in August), concerns of possibility of Chinese measures to cool property market; copper bid down $35 at $7615/ton, lead down $7 at $2235, zinc down $28 at $2145, aluminum down $7 at $2153. Gold futures pulled back as participants cashed in on its record highs recently; December Comex gold down $3 to $1,268.70/oz. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2214 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ swap rates lower after RBNZ keeps rates on hold at 3.00% but comments more downbeat than market was expecting, says local bond trader; "Everyone was expecting the no change. What did surprise the markets was that the comments suggested he (RBNZ governor Bollard) is going to be on hold for longer and his interest rate track also supported that." Tips swaps to remain around these levels for rest of session; "We have adjusted down and I don't think we will do anything else today. That is going to be it." 2-year swaps down 8 bps at 3.77%, 10-year swaps down 3 bps at 4.97%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 1856ET ]\par \par \uc2 2158 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: U.S. stocks up Wednesday on encouraging forecasts from \uc2 Kraft Foods\uc2  and McKesson boosted outlook for corporate sector; DJIA +0.4%, S&P 500 +0.4%, Nasdaq +0.5%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. Kraft +1.7%, pharma supplier McKessons +5.4%. "Companies are indicating that business continues to be as strong as they expected going into the third quarter," says Tim Evnin at Evercore Wealth Management; "I would be hesitant to say all the clouds have cleared, but the sentiment appears to be better among (corporate) management." \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2  down 4.5% after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  cuts rating to neutral from buy, says while margins should improve in 1H fiscal 2011, DRAM chip fundamentals to be less favorable beginning in 4Q10. \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  +4.7%, as says it's not cusp of selling its stake in Alibaba; earlier, Susquehanna research note had suggested sale might be "imminent." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2156 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares likely to gain, tracking offshore markets, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird. News boards of Pyne Gould (PGC.NZ), MARAC, CBS Canterbury and Southern Cross Building Society (SCBS) formally committed to proceed with merger to create NZ-listed banking, financial services group positive for market. RBNZ's decision to keep interest rates on hold also positive as low-interest rate environment makes equities more compelling but muted outlook may also weigh, in particular on retail stocks like The Warehouse (WHS.NZ), Kathmandu (KMD.NZ). NZX-50 closed flat at 3195.28 yesterday. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2155 GMT [Dow Jones] Short-term to see NZD/USD temporarily fall below 0.7200 to the 30-day moving average of 0.7162 as New Zealand interest rate markets adjust lower, says Richard Grace, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at CBA. Says there is likely to be further declines in the New Zealand six-month OIS rate, which has always been a good guide to NZD strength. But adds not to get too bearish on the NZD, given the low volatility environment and the relative yield pick-up still offered by New Zealand term-rates. Notes there is certainly more upside in AUD/NZD on a medium-term basis because of the clear guidance the RBNZ has delivered on the interest rate outlook, compared with the upside risks to the RBA. Likely AUD/NZD will move above 1.3000 in coming weeks. AUD/NZD now 1.2897. NZD/USD now 0.7267. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 1856ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
