\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Summary Of US Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 23:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2158 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: U.S. stocks up Wednesday on encouraging forecasts from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kraft Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  and McKesson boosted outlook for corporate sector; DJIA +0.4%, S&P 500 +0.4%, Nasdaq +0.5%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. Kraft +1.7%, pharma supplier McKessons +5.4%. "Companies are indicating that business continues to be as strong as they expected going into the third quarter," says Tim Evnin at Evercore Wealth Management; "I would be hesitant to say all the clouds have cleared, but the sentiment appears to be better among (corporate) management." \f28 \uc2 \f2 Micron Technology\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 4.5% after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  cuts rating to neutral from buy, says while margins should improve in 1H fiscal 2011, DRAM chip fundamentals to be less favorable beginning in 4Q10. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  +4.7%, as says it's not cusp of selling its stake in Alibaba; earlier, Susquehanna research note had suggested sale might be "imminent." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2156 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares likely to gain, tracking offshore markets, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird. News boards of Pyne Gould (PGC.NZ), MARAC, CBS Canterbury and Southern Cross Building Society (SCBS) formally committed to proceed with merger to create NZ-listed banking, financial services group positive for market. RBNZ's decision to keep interest rates on hold also positive as low-interest rate environment makes equities more compelling but muted outlook may also weigh, in particular on retail stocks like The Warehouse (WHS.NZ), Kathmandu (KMD.NZ). NZX-50 closed flat at 3195.28 yesterday. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2155 GMT [Dow Jones] Short-term to see NZD/USD temporarily fall below 0.7200 to the 30-day moving average of 0.7162 as New Zealand interest rate markets adjust lower, says Richard Grace, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at CBA. Says there is likely to be further declines in the New Zealand six-month OIS rate, which has always been a good guide to NZD strength. But adds not to get too bearish on the NZD, given the low volatility environment and the relative yield pick-up still offered by New Zealand term-rates. Notes there is certainly more upside in AUD/NZD on a medium-term basis because of the clear guidance the RBNZ has delivered on the interest rate outlook, compared with the upside risks to the RBA. Likely AUD/NZD will move above 1.3000 in coming weeks. AUD/NZD now 1.2897. NZD/USD now 0.7267. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2147 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ predicts weaker consumer spending in period ahead, which is a further reason to expect rates in New Zealand are on hold for now, says Katie Dean, senior economist at ANZ. With a goods and services tax rise also imminent, it is another nail in the coffin of a rate hike. Says RBNZ clearly remains cautious and watchful. Dean says rates in New Zealand remain very expansionary at 3.0%, which is appropriate given the conditions, which have recently included a severe earthquake near Christchurch. AUD/NZD sharply higher. Now 1.2903, up from 1.2820 before report. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2132 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ leans to the dovish side in its statement, which left OCR unchanged at 3.0%, says Phil Burke, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . Says looks now as if RBNZ will remain on hold for the remainder of 2010. Expects NZD crosses to remain under pressure with focus on the RBNZ's caution surrounding the impact on GDP from the recent earthquake in the south island. Notes AUD/NZD sharply higher. Now 1.2897, up from 1.2820 before the report. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2127 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ cuts sharply its projections for 90 day bank bill rate, now expecting rates to trade at 4.6% in 4Q 2012 from previously forecast 6.1%. Tempering expectations reflects more subdued tone in Bank's policy statement where it hints any future policy tightening will likely be at a slower pace than it expected as recently as June. Expects bills to trade 3.4% 4Q 2010, down from 3.7% previously expected. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2123 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ leaves OCR at 3.0%, as expected but decidedly cools its heels on projections for pace of any future tightening, citing impact of Canterbury earthquake and uncertain outlook for domestic, world growth. Governor Bollard spells out whilst there may be some price pressures on back of earthquake, he is prepared to look through this and seems much more concerned with slowing domestic demand. "With continued soft demand for credit, this suggests household spending will not increase to the extent previously projected." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2120 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ retains bias to hike further but clearly it's in no mood to pull the trigger given that authorities are battling a weak economy and a recent severe earthquake in Christchurch, says Su-Lin Ong, senior economist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . RBC has the next RBNZ hike in December, but Ong says that scenario is looking shaky and she would not be surprised to see the RBNZ remain sidelined until 2011. "This hiking cycle is going to be punctuated by pauses," says Ong. New Zealand OCR to stay well below neutral, which she pegs at close to 5.0%, for some time. NZD/USD slides sharply on the report. Now 0.7276, down from around 0.7310 just before RBNZ statement. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2039 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD last 0.7316 as market waits for RBNZ; "I think the RBNZ don't have a choice this morning, they will have to leave rates unchanged," says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  Director of Trading Mike Hollows. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take into account recent events like earthquake in Canterbury region; "Right here, right now, the RBNZ will probably sit on its hands." Says overnight Fonterra auction largely neutral as high NZD offsets 1.4% gain in average whole-milk powder prices. Tips support to hold at 0.7262, resistance at 0.7355. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 4:24 (Dow Jones) The main reason small businesses aren't hiring is fairly straightforward: the demand just isn't there. "If you ask businesses -- as opposed to their lobbyists -- what their problem is, you find no hint of the stories the usual suspects are telling you about government interference, political uncertainty, etc.," Paul Krugman writes at Conscience of a Liberal. "Businesses aren't hiring because of poor sales, period, end of story," he adds. "And the best thing government could do to help business would be to spend more, increasing demand. The fact that it's not going to happen doesn't change the fact that it's the simple truth." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Call us at (415) 439-6402 or email andrew.morse@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Visit the Market Talk website at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] Longest-term Tsy futures prices sharply lower as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely means BOJ won't park US dollars it bought in long-term Tsys. Profit-taking also weighs on prices and perceived lack of urgency for Fed to enact next round of quantitative easing. Dec 30-yr Tsy bonds settled 29/32 lower at 130-21. Dec ultra long Tsy bonds down 1 30/32 at 136-30. Dec 10-yr Tsy notes wound up 8/32 lower at 124-06. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2003 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD is ending higher, around 1.0263 vs 1.0243 late Tues, as CAD falls back into its recent trading ranges, sidelined as a massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities sent USD sharply higher vs JPY. There are no significant Canadian data releases for the rest of the week. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 1758ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
