\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: RBNZ Cools Its Heels On Rate Hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,241 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 23:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2123 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ leaves OCR at 3.0%, as expected but decidedly cools its heels on projections for pace of any future tightening, citing impact of Canterbury earthquake and uncertain outlook for domestic, world growth. Governor Bollard spells out whilst there may be some price pressures on back of earthquake, he is prepared to look through this and seems much more concerned with slowing domestic demand. "With continued soft demand for credit, this suggests household spending will not increase to the extent previously projected." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2120 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ retains bias to hike further but clearly it's in no mood to pull the trigger given that authorities are battling a weak economy and a recent severe earthquake in Christchurch, says Su-Lin Ong, senior economist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . RBC has the next RBNZ hike in December, but Ong says that scenario is looking shaky and she would not be surprised to see the RBNZ remain sidelined until 2011. "This hiking cycle is going to be punctuated by pauses," says Ong. New Zealand OCR to stay well below neutral, which she pegs at close to 5.0%, for some time. NZD/USD slides sharply on the report. Now 0.7276, down from around 0.7310 just before RBNZ statement. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2039 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD last 0.7316 as market waits for RBNZ; "I think the RBNZ don't have a choice this morning, they will have to leave rates unchanged," says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  Director of Trading Mike Hollows. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take into account recent events like earthquake in Canterbury region; "Right here, right now, the RBNZ will probably sit on its hands." Says overnight Fonterra auction largely neutral as high NZD offsets 1.4% gain in average whole-milk powder prices. Tips support to hold at 0.7262, resistance at 0.7355. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 4:24 (Dow Jones) The main reason small businesses aren't hiring is fairly straightforward: the demand just isn't there. "If you ask businesses -- as opposed to their lobbyists -- what their problem is, you find no hint of the stories the usual suspects are telling you about government interference, political uncertainty, etc.," Paul Krugman writes at Conscience of a Liberal. "Businesses aren't hiring because of poor sales, period, end of story," he adds. "And the best thing government could do to help business would be to spend more, increasing demand. The fact that it's not going to happen doesn't change the fact that it's the simple truth." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Call us at (415) 439-6402 or email andrew.morse@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Visit the Market Talk website at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] Longest-term Tsy futures prices sharply lower as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely means BOJ won't park US dollars it bought in long-term Tsys. Profit-taking also weighs on prices and perceived lack of urgency for Fed to enact next round of quantitative easing. Dec 30-yr Tsy bonds settled 29/32 lower at 130-21. Dec ultra long Tsy bonds down 1 30/32 at 136-30. Dec 10-yr Tsy notes wound up 8/32 lower at 124-06. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2003 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD is ending higher, around 1.0263 vs 1.0243 late Tues, as CAD falls back into its recent trading ranges, sidelined as a massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities sent USD sharply higher vs JPY. There are no significant Canadian data releases for the rest of the week. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1957 GMT [Dow Jones] After bouncing back and forth Wed, fed-funds futures find equilibrium at Tues' level -- again pricing in 46% chance for FOMC to begin raising funds rate in 3Q 2011. Oct '11 contract priced in 62% chance for 0.5% rate at Mon's settlement, and 76% chance Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT [Dow Jones] Front-end Eurodollar futures unchanged, and longer-dated prices a little lower on some dueling market forces. Support from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as BOJ expected to park the dollars purchased in more liquid/less volatile short-end of the curve. Higher stocks considered a pressuring factor as there's less demand for safety. Dec '10 through Dec '11 unchanged to up 0.5 BP. Mar '12 through Dec '13 unchanged to down 4 BP. Economic worries persist as nearest 1-yr calendar spread narrows in recent days. Dec '11 prices in rate 42.5 BP above rate priced into Dec '10. That's unchanged from Tues, but down from 52 BP Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT [Dow Jones] North Carolina's State Education Assistance Authority is tapping the asset-backed bond market with a $908M student loan-backed security, according to a preliminary prospectus. Lead managers on the bond are \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  and JPM. The bond is one of several issued this week, after the ABS market saw a three-week pause during the last few weeks of summer.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1950 GMT [Dow Jone