\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZD/USD Steady, RBNZ Tipped To Pause - \uc2 HIFX\b0\par\par\uc2 1,249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2039 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD last 0.7316 as market waits for RBNZ; "I think the RBNZ don't have a choice this morning, they will have to leave rates unchanged," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 HIFX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Director of Trading Mike Hollows. Says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will take into account recent events like earthquake in Canterbury region; "Right here, right now, the RBNZ will probably sit on its hands." Says overnight Fonterra auction largely neutral as high NZD offsets 1.4% gain in average whole-milk powder prices. Tips support to hold at 0.7262, resistance at 0.7355. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 4:24 (Dow Jones) The main reason small businesses aren't hiring is fairly straightforward: the demand just isn't there. "If you ask businesses -- as opposed to their lobbyists -- what their problem is, you find no hint of the stories the usual suspects are telling you about government interference, political uncertainty, etc.," Paul Krugman writes at Conscience of a Liberal. "Businesses aren't hiring because of poor sales, period, end of story," he adds. "And the best thing government could do to help business would be to spend more, increasing demand. The fact that it's not going to happen doesn't change the fact that it's the simple truth." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/09/15/its-demand-stupid/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Call us at (415) 439-6402 or email andrew.morse@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Visit the Market Talk website at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2008 GMT [Dow Jones] Longest-term Tsy futures prices sharply lower as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely means BOJ won't park US dollars it bought in long-term Tsys. Profit-taking also weighs on prices and perceived lack of urgency for Fed to enact next round of quantitative easing. Dec 30-yr Tsy bonds settled 29/32 lower at 130-21. Dec ultra long Tsy bonds down 1 30/32 at 136-30. Dec 10-yr Tsy notes wound up 8/32 lower at 124-06. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2003 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD is ending higher, around 1.0263 vs 1.0243 late Tues, as CAD falls back into its recent trading ranges, sidelined as a massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities sent USD sharply higher vs JPY. There are no significant Canadian data releases for the rest of the week. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1957 GMT [Dow Jones] After bouncing back and forth Wed, fed-funds futures find equilibrium at Tues' level -- again pricing in 46% chance for FOMC to begin raising funds rate in 3Q 2011. Oct '11 contract priced in 62% chance for 0.5% rate at Mon's settlement, and 76% chance Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT [Dow Jones] Front-end Eurodollar futures unchanged, and longer-dated prices a little lower on some dueling market forces. Support from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as BOJ expected to park the dollars purchased in more liquid/less volatile short-end of the curve. Higher stocks considered a pressuring factor as there's less demand for safety. Dec '10 through Dec '11 unchanged to up 0.5 BP. Mar '12 through Dec '13 unchanged to down 4 BP. Economic worries persist as nearest 1-yr calendar spread narrows in recent days. Dec '11 prices in rate 42.5 BP above rate priced into Dec '10. That's unchanged from Tues, but down from 52 BP Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT [Dow Jones] North Carolina's State Education Assistance Authority is tapping the asset-backed bond market with a $908M student loan-backed security, according to a preliminary prospectus. Lead managers on the bond are \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  and JPM. The bond is one of several issued this week, after the ABS market saw a three-week pause during the last few weeks of summer.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1950 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD treading water as markets await RBNZ Monetary Policy Statement, OCR review at 2100 GMT, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Pair last 0.7318 vs 0.7325 late yesterday in NZ; "Expect an underperforming NZD as a result of no change to the cash rate and a subdued statement accompanying this." Says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  facing difficult balance act, will need to acknowledge recent events while taking on board forward looking scenario; "NZD should under perform as a result of their accompanying statement although yields will still remain attractive relative to many." Tips a range of 0.7305-0.7395. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1936 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wrap: The yen plummeted against the dollar Wednesday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in more than six years. The greenback gained more than 3% after on Tuesday it hit a 15-year low against the yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again Thursday if necessary. The euro ticked down only slightly against the dollar, likely because speculation still swirls as to whether the Federal Reserve will initiate another round of asset purchases to kickstart a moribund U.S. economy, said Vasilli Serebriakov of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Talk of further Fed action has weighed on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1919 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan has decided not to sterilize the funds used to sell JPY and to instead let them increase the money supply, and by doing so, Japanese officials may actually get more "bang for their buck" out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this time than in the '03-04 episode, says Montreal-based National Bank Financial. Its forecast is for USD/JPY to rise to 90 over the next three months. The liquidity injection by the BOJ is a positive to the extent that it reduces deflationary pressures and may prod China to let its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate more rapidly, NBF says. "China remains the key to a realignment of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," it adds. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1915 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ likely to take breather in tightening cycle at today's monetary policy review and keep Official Cash Rate at 3.00%, says Westpac Bank; "While a pause itself should not be a significant surprise, the market will want to know the expected duration of such. For clues on that front, the one-page media statement and the detailed Monetary Policy Statement will be finely combed." Tips \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to say something along lines of "given recent developments, we believe it is appropriate to leave current policy settings in place for the time being." However, also expects bank to say further removal of monetary policy stimulus will be necessary if the economy evolves as projected. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1858 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports that Santander Chile's new global bond deal, rated Aa3 by the company, will have three tranches that add up to a total of $1.2 billion. The first tranche, to be issued and payable in US dollars, will be $500 million and mature in 2015. The second tranche, to be issued in Chilean pesos and payable in US dollars, will amount to an equivalent of $500 million and mature in 2020. The third tranche will consist of a floating-rate note with maturity of one year in the amount of $200 million. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 1639ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000e7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
