\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Plummets Vs. Rivals After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 800 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 22:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)-- The yen plummeted against the dollar Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 The dollar gained more than 3% against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after having dropped Tuesday to its lowest level in 15 years vs. the yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Thursday in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Concerns over the pace of the U.S. recovery have recently sent investors flocking to the perceived safety of the yen, especially as investors speculate whether the Federal Reserve could implement another round of asset purchases to kick-start a moribund economy, which would likely weigh further on the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might temporarily push the dollar higher against the yen, but is unlikely to have a longer-term effect, said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's [recent] moves are really mostly to do with what's happening in the U.S.," Serebriakov said of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. Until the U.S. notches a more solid recovery, "the yen's medium-term trend will be driven by the U.S. economic and monetary-policy developments," he said, questioning the lasting effectiveness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the dollar dropped Tuesday to the 15-year low of Y82.87, the initial barrage of yen-selling orders hit the market early in the Asian day; it was followed by bursts that pushed the dollar above Y85.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight announced the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  about 20 minutes after the first sales hit the market. The wave of yen selling was estimated at Y1 trillion to counter what the government called "severe economic conditions" facing the country.\par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the dollar was at Y85.63 from late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG, slightly off its Y85.78 high. The dollar remained stuck around Y85.60 through most of the North American session, with no spikes higher during New York trading that would indicate direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "They could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  via stealth" measures, but "we're not seeing anything on the charts," or in the bank's own flows, during North American trading, said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Stamford, Conn.\par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, ticked down only slightly against the dollar, trading at $1.3012 from $1.3020 late Tuesday. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also was sharply higher against the yen, at Y111.36 from Y108.20. The U.K. pound was at $1.5623 from $1.5563. The dollar was at CHF1.0035 from CHF0.9950.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was up at 81.453 from 81.074.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  through a spokesman Wednesday declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. Treasury, asked overnight Tuesday if it had been consulted or had in any way participated in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also declined comment.\par \par \uc2 To see the dollar's performance against the yen, please see:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4599"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4599}}}\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on a day when Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  restarted buying U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a move designed to halt the ascent of the peso, which threatens to undermine some key sectors of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Separately, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the yuan higher against the U.S. dollar for the fourth consecutive trading session Wednesday. Shortly after the start of the trading, the yuan was at 6.7330 to the dollar, its strongest level since it began trading in 1994. At that level, the yuan was up 1.4% against the dollar since June 19, when China dropped the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 23-month-long de facto peg to the U.S. unit and pledged to make the yuan more flexible and market oriented. The dollar ended at CNY6.7422 Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble also fell to a multi-month low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, despite relatively steady oil prices.\par \par \uc2 With the ICE Dollar Index strengthening, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bearish exchange-traded fund was down 0.38% from late Tuesday, while its PowerShares U.S. Dollar Index Bullish was up 0.43%. The two exchange-traded funds are based on \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures indexes, whose composition mirrors that of the ICE's Dollar Index.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Andrew J. Johnson in New York and Takashi Nakamichi and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this article.) [ 09-15-10 1612ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000dz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
