\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Yen Falls Hard After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,316 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 21:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1936 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wrap: The yen plummeted against the dollar Wednesday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in more than six years. The greenback gained more than 3% after on Tuesday it hit a 15-year low against the yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again Thursday if necessary. The euro ticked down only slightly against the dollar, likely because speculation still swirls as to whether the Federal Reserve will initiate another round of asset purchases to kickstart a moribund U.S. economy, said Vasilli Serebriakov of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Talk of further Fed action has weighed on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1919 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan has decided not to sterilize the funds used to sell JPY and to instead let them increase the money supply, and by doing so, Japanese officials may actually get more "bang for their buck" out of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this time than in the '03-04 episode, says Montreal-based National Bank Financial. Its forecast is for USD/JPY to rise to 90 over the next three months. The liquidity injection by the BOJ is a positive to the extent that it reduces deflationary pressures and may prod China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate more rapidly, NBF says. "China remains the key to a realignment of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it adds. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1915 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ likely to take breather in tightening cycle at today's monetary policy review and keep Official Cash Rate at 3.00%, says Westpac Bank; "While a pause itself should not be a significant surprise, the market will want to know the expected duration of such. For clues on that front, the one-page media statement and the detailed Monetary Policy Statement will be finely combed." Tips \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to say something along lines of "given recent developments, we believe it is appropriate to leave current policy settings in place for the time being." However, also expects bank to say further removal of monetary policy stimulus will be necessary if the economy evolves as projected. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1858 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Moody's\uc2  reports that Santander Chile's new global bond deal, rated Aa3 by the company, will have three tranches that add up to a total of $1.2 billion. The first tranche, to be issued and payable in US dollars, will be $500 million and mature in 2015. The second tranche, to be issued in Chilean pesos and payable in US dollars, will amount to an equivalent of $500 million and mature in 2020. The third tranche will consist of a floating-rate note with maturity of one year in the amount of $200 million. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1900 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: RBNZ widely expected to keep Official Cash Rate unchanged at 3.0% later, while in statement highlighting uncertain economic outlook, particularly after Christchurch earthquake earlier this month (Treasury says quake will reduce GDP by 0.4% in September quarter.) In Dow Jones poll of 12 economists, 4 say there's 95% chance RBI will hold while 6 tip 90% chance of hold; remaining 2 both tip over 60% chance of RBI standing pat. "There will be a greater-than-usual degree of uncertainty in the RBNZ's economic projections and their 90-day profile. We expect this message to be emphasized in the OCR assessment, with the RBNZ committing to stimulatory policy for longer relative to their June assessment," says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . In July, RBNZ increased OCR by 25 bps to 3.00%, second consecutive hike after staying on sidelines for more than year. (rebbeca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1857 GMT [Dow Jones] Mortgage buyers have started coming back on lower prices and wider risk premiums. However, most portfolio managers and tradtional buyers remain cautious as the drop in the refi index throws off weeks of gains, and confuses the picture of a pick up in prepayment activity. Agency mortgage securities are a tad wider at 140 bps over comparable Tsy yields. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1826 GMT [Dow Jones] Amidst commotion of JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and plotting what may happen next if the measures done so far don't take, GBP/USD has quietly strengthened. The pair at 1.5641 continues to trade within striking distance of its intra-day high of 1.5652, up from 1.5563 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. Investors may be using the pair to express a negative on the greenback, which offered fresh evidence of sluggishness with weak industrial production numbers. Investors have two ways to play against the dollar with the EUR and GBP, but sterling isn't a bad pick especially in light of its "fiscal consolidation" measures, writes Jens Nordvig of Nomura. "For this reason, we think GBP/USD is the better expression of USD bearish views at this point," he said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1743 GMT [Dow Jones] Supply of new corporate bonds further sparks choppy trade in the Treasury market. The long end falls as companies sell Treasurys to hedge rate risks for new corp bond supply. The selling also forces many shorter-dated Treasurys to give up early gains. 10-year note's price is 9/32 lower to yield 2.717%, the 30-year bond is the biggest loser, falling 1 7/32 to yield 3.870%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1729 GMT [Dow Jones] Today the US Treasury got a gift: a future guaranteed buyer of at least $300 billion more in bonds, says ZeroHedge about yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues debt, raises Yen, uses Yen to buy Dollars, uses Dollars to buy Treasuries, completing the ponzi loop whereby it has just added to its debt, using the proceeds to fund America's endless credit fueled spending spree." (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.zerohedge.com/article/observations-how-much-capacity-intervention-does-boj-has-and-whether-intervention-will-be-st?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+zerohedge%2Ffeed+%28zero+hedge+-+on+a+long+enough+timeline%2C+the+survival+rate+for+everyone+drops+to+zero%29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.zerohedge.com/article/observations-how-much-capacity-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 -does-boj-has-and-whether-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 -will-be-st?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+zerohedge%2Ffeed+%28zero+hedge+-+on+a+long+enough+timeline%2C+the+survival+rate+for+everyone+drops+to+zero%29}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1705 GMT [Dow Jones] Brazilian steelmaker CSN (SID, CSNA3.BR) has received nearly $7 billion in orders for its perpetual bond offer, set to price later Wednesday, according to a person familiar with the matter. Size of the deal hasn't been announced but market participants expecting $1 billion sale. Yield also likely to come in at the low end of the guidance for 7%-7.25%, the person said. (kejal.vyas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1702 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cliffs Natural Resources\f28 \uc2 \f2  $1B (upsized from $750 mln) 2-part bond offer has launched with a $500 mln 10-yr tranche at 210 bps over Tsys and a $500 mln 30-yr tranche at 250 bps over, according to a person familiar with the deal, with each tranche coming 5 bps tighter than initial price guidance. Via BAML and JPM Morgan. Proceeds will be used to repay credit revolver borrowings and for general corporate purposes. (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1657 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 CoreLogic\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist says the overall housing picture looks similar to prices back in November 2009, when housing was on a decline. \f28 \uc2 \f2 CoreLogic\f28 \uc2 \f2  home price index was flat for July, with no change compared to July 2009, the first time in 5 months that no gains were reported. "The majority of states experienced price declines, and these are spreading across more geographies relative to a few months ago," said economist Mark Fleming. (prabha.natarajan@dowjones.com [ 09-15-10 1537ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000dj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
