\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar soars, yen drops as  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  for first time in 6 yrs\par \par \uc2 * Fin Min Noda says will continue to take decisive action\par \par \uc2 * BOJ detected buying dollars intermittently - traders\par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped two yen from a 15-year low after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for the first time in six years, in a move traders said would only buy it time in slowing the yen's persistent rise.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped send the euro, Australian dollar and sterling 2 percent higher on the day against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it was not clear whether Japan had bought anything other than dollars.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the impact of the yen's rise on the economy could not be ignored and that Japan would continue to take action, but that it had been acting solo.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders said continued in the Asian session after the initial bout at around 83.00 yen per dollar which came shortly after the dollar hit a 15-year low of 82.87 yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 2.4 percent to 85.05 yen <JPY=>, while the euro climbed 2.2 percent to 110.34 yen <EURJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 Traders said Japan may want to bring the dollar up to around 85 yen.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is likely trying to bring dollar/yen above 84.50-70 as chart-wise, if the pair stops below that level, it would be pushed back down," said a trader at a Japanese trust bank.\par \par \uc2 "The immediate impact from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks very big, but then the yen will likely eventually go back to the strong side as the actual problem exists in the dollar."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan started buying the dollar from around 10:30 a.m. (0130 GMT). It also said it would provide ample funds to markets while keeping very easy monetary conditions.\par \par \uc2 The 85 yen could be a target for Tokyo as many Japanese exporters want to sell the dollar above that level before their half-year book-closing at the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 It was \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, when it capped a 15-month campaign to sell the yen. Then it sold a total of 35 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would take decisive steps if necessary, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves closely.\par \par \uc2 But few in the market expect a campaign of the same magnitude as 2003-2004, although traders say Japan is likely to keep selling the yen, should it threaten to rise, at least until the end of this month.\par \par \uc2 Many traders' initial take on Wednesday's action was that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be more tactical in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than defending a line in the sand at 83 yen.\par \par \uc2 "Now they've done it, they won't be able to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  halfway. I don't think they have that option," said a trader at a Japanese bank.\par \par \uc2 The yen had gained fresh momentum on Tuesday after Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan won a leadership ballot against a rival seen as more willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was also hurt early on by talk that the Federal Reserve could be nearing more quantitative easing but it edged higher on the euro, which slipped 0.2 percent to $1.2978 <EUR=>.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar shot to its highest in nearly three months at 79.72 yen <AUDJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped boost the dollar against the Swiss franc to $1.0025 <CHF=> from nine-month low of 0.9933 hit on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano and Aiko Hayashi, Editing by Charlotte Cooper and Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \uc2 LANGEN|Table|GNN|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f000cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
