\par\par \b \uc2 TIMELINE- \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market, first time in 6 yrs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,213 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Sept 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday for the first time in six years, selling yen to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is threatening a fragile economic recovery. [ID:nTOE68E02W]\par \par \uc2 Here are some milestones in the yen's 138-year history:\par \par \uc2 1871 - The yen becomes Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of the Meiji Restoration, which marked the start of Japan's modernisation and opening to the rest of the world. Japan adopts the gold standard.\par \par \uc2 1949 - After World War Two the dollar's fixed rate is set at 360 yen via the Bretton Woods system, partly to help stabilise prices in the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 1959 - The dollar/yen exchange rate is liberalised. The margin of fluctuation is set at 0.5 percent on either side of its dollar parity.\par \par \uc2 1963 - The margin of fluctuation is widened to 0.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 1971 - United States abandons gold standard. The end of Bretton Woods system of fixed exchange rates forces a realignment of world \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dec. 1971 - Smithsonian Agreement sets the dollar/yen exchange rate at 308 yen, and allows it to fluctuate in a wider band between 301.07 yen and 314.93 yen.\par \par \uc2 1973 - Japanese monetary authorities decide to let the yen float freely against the dollar, and the yen appreciates as far as 263 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 1978 - The yen pushes through 200 to the dollar for the first time, strengthening as far as 177.\par \par \uc2 1980 to 1985 - Yen's appreciation halts and partially reverses despite Japan's big trade surpluses. Higher U.S. interest rates see Japanese investors put money in dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 1985 - The Group of Five industrial nations, the predecessor to the G7, sign the Plaza Accord in which they agree that the dollar is overvalued and that they will move to weaken it. The yen climbs from its pre-accord level around 240 to 211 in October and 200 in November, a 20 percent rise in just a few months.\par \par \uc2 1986 - The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls further to around 190 yen in January, 167 yen in April and 153 yen in August.\par \par \uc2 1987 - In February, six of the G7 nations sign the Louvre Accord, which aims to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and halt the dollar's broad decline. The dollar still falls from near 153 to 137 in April and 120.80 by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 1988 - On Jan. 4, the dollar falls to a post-war low of 120.45 yen in Tokyo trade, a level that holds as the low for more than five years. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars and sell yen that day on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Aug. 17, 1993 - The dollar declines to a new post-war low of 100.40 yen in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 June 21, 1994 - The dollar falls through the key 100 yen level and touches a record postwar low of 99.85 yen in New York trade before finishing at 100.30 yen.\par \par \uc2 April 19, 1995 - The dollar hits a record postwar low at 79.75 yen after U.S.-Japanese trade frictions spark heavy selling. By the end of the year it is near 103.40.\par \par \uc2 1998 - Asian financial crisis sees yen weaken to nearly 148 yen vs dollar in August, even after U.S. authorities join the Bank of Japan to buy yen, spending $833 million, in June.\par \par \uc2 In October, dollar tumbles from near 136 yen to 111.50 yen, as carry trades unwind following the near-collapse of hedge fund major \uc2 Long-Term Capital Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1999 - The yen strengthens further despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reaching 102 in November.\par \par \uc2 2001 - Following the Sept 11 attacks on the United States, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars.\par \par \uc2 2003 - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise against the dollar, partly to shield Japanese exporters as the economy remains stuck in its post-bubble slump and deflation. The MOF spends 20.4 trillion yen ($200 billion) over the year, nearly all of it to buy dollars and sell yen.\par \par \uc2 2004 - The MOF spends 14.8 trillion yen ($145 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of the year, including 1.67 trillion yen buying dollars on Jan. 9 alone. But the MOF ceases \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March and has never since resumed.\par \par \uc2 2005 - The yen hits a high of 101.67 yen in January but then falls, hitting 121.40 in December. Yen carry trades and Japanese investors shifting funds into foreign assets drive the slide.\par \par \uc2 June 2007 - The dollar hits a 4-1/2-year high of 124.14 yen.\par \par \uc2 July 2007 - Yen's broad depreciation takes it to a 22-year low on a real effective exchange rate (REER) basis. Since January 2005 the yen loses 25 percent of its value on a REER basis.\par \par \uc2 March 13, 2008 - The yen hits a 12-year high of 99.77.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 24, 2008 - Yen hits 13-year high of 90.87 vs the dollar. Also sets an all-time high of 55.11 against the Australian dollar, which loses almost a third of its value in just a month on a massive unwind of carry trades.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 27, 2008 - The yen's surge prompts the G7 to issue statement singling out the yen in warning on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Dec. 12, 2008 - The dollar falls through 90 yen for the first time in 13 years after a bill to rescue U.S. automakers fails in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 22, 2009 - Hits fresh 13-year high of 87.10 against dollar, driven up by risk aversion and option-led dollar selling.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 28, 2009 - Marks 8-month high of 88.23 against greenback, but later loses ground as Japan's finance minister tries to tone down earlier comments suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely.\par \par \uc2 Oct. 7, 2009 - Yen reaches fresh 8-month high versus the greenback of 88.01 as market players probe how far Japanese authorities will allow the yen to rise.\par \par \uc2 Nov. 27, 2009 - Hits 14-year high of 84.82 on trading platform EBS.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 11 - Dollar marks 15-year low of 84.72 yen on electronic trading platform EBS, after taking out option barriers at 85.00 and 84.75, fuelled by a narrowing of the spread between U.S. and Japanese two-year bond yields.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 24 - The dollar hits a fresh 15-year trough of 83.58 yen on EBS on fears the global economy is slowing, testing Japanese authorities' resolve to stem the yen's climb.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 8 - Yen strikes a new 15-year high against the dollar of 83.34 on EBS as concerns about euro zone banks and sovereign debt prompt a flight to safety.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 15 - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in six years, selling yen to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar hit a 15-year-low at 82.87 yen <JPY=> earlier in the day. Sources: Reuters, Bank of Japan, Bank of England (Writing by Mathew Veedon and Eric Burroughs; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YEN TIMELINE|LANGEN|NAT|J|SI|RBN|UKI|AUF|ND|ABN|M|E|D|U|G|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Weaken the Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 Media Release - NZPA   \par \uc2 1,904 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 21:53\par \uc2 Mediacom\par \uc2 NZPAME\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 New Zealand Press Association   \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Global Markets Daily: \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in USD/JPY\par \par \uc2 As USD/JPY slipped below 83, in early Asian trading, the Japanese government stepped in to weaken the Yen for the first time since March 2004. This follows increasing verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 'threatening' rhetoric in recent days.\par \par \uc2 With the re-election of Kan as DPJ leader yesterday, the timing is potentially a surprise given that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was perceived as more likely if Ozawa had taken the reigns. But this perception may have been precisely why the authorities felt they needed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to stem the risk of speculative positioning becoming increasingly on-sided following the election outcome. The \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility around the outcome of the DPJ leadership election yesterday already had this flavour.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the DPJ election, the fact that the market started focusing on potential QE2 from the Fed last night added to the pressure via interest rate differentials.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to combat Yen strength was pretty quickly confirmed by FinMin Noda, who said it could hurt the Japanese economy and financial stability. Interestingly Noda wouldn't comment on whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in USD/JPY, however the price action suggests this was likely to be the case. Noda also commented that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taken alone and bold action would be taken when needed. Commentary from a MoF official stated that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not finished, which is not particularly surprising.\par \par \uc2 In the last phase of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  running from early 2003 to March 16th 2004, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on 129 of those days, accumulating Yen36.3trn-worth of reserves in the process. The most persistent phase of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in late 2003, beginning of 2004 and the largest one day Yen selling January 9th 2004 of JPY1.6trn. Over that period, the Japanese initially defended the 116 area, before stepping away in late September, in the run-up to the Dubai G7 meeting and 'smoothing' the cross down to 105-106. This level was subsequently defended heavily. The BoJ/MoF will provide aggregate data on the size of today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and any subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the last business day on the month. Detailed daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data typically becomes available on a quarterly basis.\par \par \uc2 On the assumption that the volatility around the DPJ election result yesterday was a key trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, it is quite possible that the Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in response to similar circumstances and it is indeed possible that we see more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the next few days. Broadly speaking, the administration likely want to introduce more two-way risk in USD/JPY in order to stem further speculatively Yen appreciation vs the USD or on a TWI basis. Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, we would not rule out notable new lows in $/JPY at this stage. Importantly, the political environment is unlikely to tolerate persistent Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the broader political pressures to allow Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.\par \par \uc2 BoJ Governor Shirakawa commented that he hopes MoF action will stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and that the BoJ will continue to supply ample liquidity to the markets and pursue strong monetary easing. This suggests that the BoJ are not in a rush to mop up the liquidity provided by today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As a reminder, in an emergency meeting on August 30th the BoJ announced it would start providing 6-month term funding of approximately JPY10trn.\par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands in the middle of other macro events that have the potential to push $/JPY around. We have long highlighted that USD/JPY has a close relationship with 2-yr swap differentials. Admittedly, this relationship has 'broken down' since the beginning of August, possibly due to fiscal half-yearend repatriation flows by Japanese exporters. However, interest rates will likely still matter. We expect the Fed to undertake QE2 most likely at the November meeting and this has the potential to cause US fixed income to rally. Indeed, QE2 caught the market's attention in New York trading yesterday despite better than expected US retail sales data and that caused US yields to fall (which also pushed the Dollar lower). A rally in US rates is likely to weaken the Dollar and by implication put renewed appreciation pressure on the Yen. Given that both the US and Japan are going down the path of unconventional monetary easing, the most credible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to 'win' the weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Today's Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has occurred against the backdrop of political disapproval of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indeed, we think that the recent notable shifts in the CNY fix reflect political pressure on the Chinese administration to allow a stronger CNY. We also believe this pressure is not just focused on the Chinese, but also on other administrations in the region which have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily and indeed we have a trade recommendation to be short INR/KRW to capture this theme. With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese, which occurred after the PBOC fix today, it will be extremely interesting to see how the Chinese react in tomorrow's fix. The Japanese moves may also strengthen the resolve of the likes of KRW to smooth heavily even in the face of political pressure. Given the G20 have expressed disapproval of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manipulate \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so commentary on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from this body will be interesting to watch. As it stands, we think that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not be endorsed and pressure on the rest of the region to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain.\par \par \uc2 On the back of the sharp Yen weakness, the Nikkei jumped by 2.7%, which marks a notable outperformance of the rest of the region which is up about half a percent. 10-yr JGBs rallied by 9bps, presumably reflecting the injection of liquidity. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  all weakened in response with the most notable weakness in KRW, MYR, THB and SGD. The major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are pretty much unmoved.\par \par \uc2 2. What else is going on? While today's markets will remain focused on $/JPY, there are other items on the agenda. On the data front, US releases are probably key and bring industrial production, where we expect an above consensus 0.3%mom rise, and MBA mortgage applications. We also get the US Empire survey which will provide the latest snippet on business sentiment in the US. The consensus is looking for a small rise from 7.1 to 8.0. That said, in NY trading yesterday, the market started to latch on to the prospect of QE2 and thus ignored the better than expected retail sales data. In our view, QE2 is most likely to occur later this year or early next, with the November meeting as our base case.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the US data, the UK will publish the next batch of labour market data. In Asian time, continued weakness in NZ credit card spending re-highlighted the weakness of the consumer and we expect the RBNZ to keep rates on hold at their coming meetings and to issue a rather cautious statement. In Australia, consumer confidence weakened somewhat on the month, but remains at relatively elevated levels. Despite the decline in consumer confidence and the overnight move in US rates, 5-yr Aussie swap rates climbed higher to 5.38% to the benefit of our paying recommendation. We are raising stops from 4.80% to 5.20%.\par \par \uc2 3. Current Trading Views The following trading ideas from the Global Markets Group reflect shorter-term views, which may differ from the longer-term "structural" positions included in our "Top Trades" list further below.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : 1. Stay short $/CNY via 1-yr NDFs, opened at 6.7550 on 10 June 2010, with a target of 6.50, and a 1-day stop of 6.83, now at 6.6822.\par \par \uc2 2. Close short MXN/CLP, opened at 41.19 on 05 August 2010, for a potential gain of 6.2%.\par \par \uc2 3. Stay short EUR/AUD, opened at 1.3750 on 9 September 2010, with a target of 1.30 and a one-day stop on a close above 1.4150, currently at 1.3846.\par \par \uc2 4. Stay short INR/KRW, opened at 25.11 on 13 September 2010, with a target of 23 and a stop on a close above 26, now at 25.0383.\par \par \uc2 On Rates: 1. Stay short an equally weighted basket of 5-yr CDS spreads in Poland, Korea, China and Czech, opened at an average spread of 115 bp on 17 June 2010, with an initial target of 75 bp and a stop-loss of 140 bp, now at 103.0 bp.\par \par \uc2 2. Stay short UK inflation vs. long Euro-zone inflation through 5-yr zero coupon swaps, opened on 09 July 2010 at 1.63%, for a target of 1.2% and a close set at 1.85%, now at 1.42%.\par \par \uc2 3. Stay short 5-yr AUD swaps vs. 6-month rates, opened at 5.09 on 03 September 2010, for a target of 5.60 and a stop on a close below 5.20 (raised from 4.80), now at 5.35.\par \par \uc2 4. Hold steepeners in EUR 10s-30s vs. flatteners in GBP 10s-30s, opened at -35bp on 03 September 2010, for a target of 0 bp and a stop on a close below -50 bp, now at -32.9 bp.\par \par \uc2 5. Stay long 10-yr Italy vs. France, opened at 112 bp on 03 September 2010, for a target of 70 bp and a stop on a close above 130 bp, now at 108.8 bp.\par \par \uc2 6. Stay long 30-yr Greek GGBs, opened at 54c (ask) on 03 September 2010, for a target of 60c and a stop on a close below 50c, now at 55.20c (bid).\par \par \uc2 Equity Trading Strategies: 1. Go long EEM/SPX, opened at 0.0386 on 14 September 2010, with a target of +9% and a stop of -4%, now at 0.0386.\par \par \uc2 4. Recommended Top Trades for 2010 (opened on 02 December 2009 unless otherwise stated)\par \par \uc2 1. Stay short S&P 500 Dec10/Dec11 Forward Starting Variance Swap, opened at 28.20, with a target of 21, now at 29.85.\par \par \uc2 2. Stay long Russian Equities (RDXUSD), opened at 1645.9 for a target of 2050, now at 1642.38.\par \par \uc2 3. Stay long GBP/NZD, opened at 2.29, with a target of 2.60, now at 2.1112.\par \par \uc2 4. Close short 2-yr GBP swap rates vs. long 2-yr AUD swap rates on a 1-yr forward basis, opened at -268.5 bp, for a potential loss of 24 bp (inclusive of carry).\par \par \uc2 5. Close short 2-yr TRY rates through cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, opened at 8.77%, with a target of 12.0%, for a potential loss of 168 bp (inclusive of carry).\par \par \uc2 6. Close long 5yr credit protection in Spain vs. short 5yr credit protection in Ireland at 13 bp, opened at 70 bp, with a target of 20 bp, for a potential profit of 2.9% (inclusive of carry).\par \par \uc2 7. Stay long the GS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Growth Current, opened at 103.5, with a target of 111.8, now at 104.8.\par \par \uc2 8. Stay long PLN/JPY, opened at 32.1, with a target of 37.5, now at 28.1339.\par \par \uc2 9. Stay long Chinese Equities (HSCEI), opened at 12616.01 on 01 April 2010, with a target of 15000, now at 12048.27.\par \par \uc2 5661464   \par \par \uc2 Dokument NZPAME0020100916e69f0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
