\par\par \b \uc2 OIL FUTURES: Crude Falls On Dollar Strength; EIA Data May Support\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mari Iwata   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 08:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Crude oil futures fell Wednesday in Asia, extending overnight losses on the back of a stronger dollar in Asian morning trade.\par \par \uc2 The decline leveled off in the Asian afternoon as market participants awaited U.S. inventory data due at 1430 GMT that could show a draw in crude oil stocks and lend support to prices, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 On the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 , light, sweet crude futures for delivery in October traded at $76.39 a barrel at 0624 GMT, down $0.41 in the Globex electronic session. October Brent crude on London's ICE Futures exchange fell $0.11 to $79.05 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices were mixed in early Asian hours in the absence of clear direction, with near-month contracts falling and forward months trading higher, but once the dollar gained against the yen after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy greenbacks, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' value against the dollar fell also, triggering a broad-based selloff of many commodities, including crude oil.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda confirmed the USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday morning--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo traders were "buying yen-priced commodities and selling dollar-denominated commodities to take advantage of a cheaper yen," said Hitoshi Inagawa, trader of broker Yutaka Shoji.\par \par \uc2 In the late afternoon, traders shifted focus to the U.S. Energy Information Administration's oil data. If the EIA numbers are in line with data issued Tuesday by the American Petroleum Institute that showed a surprising 3.3 million-barrel build in crude inventories, support could melt away in view of the average 2.5-million barrel draw estimated by analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Market sentiment isn't particularly dented by the API data, however, since the EIA data aren't always in line with API's, Newedge Japan trader Ryoma Furumi said. "If today's EIA report shows a large drop in crude stockpiles as expected, it may trigger a buying spree," he noted.\par \par \uc2 Nymex reformulated gasoline blendstock for October--the benchmark gasoline contract--fell 75 points to $1.9615 a gallon, while October heating oil traded at $2.1265, 23 points lower.\par \par \uc2 ICE gasoil for October changed hands at $674.25 a metric ton, down $3.00 from Tuesday's settlement.\par \par \uc2 By Mari Iwata, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2798; mari.iwata@dowjones.com [ 09-15-10 0225ET ]\par \par \uc2 1086 28028 28031 4107 75024   \par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020100915e69f0000q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
