\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan manuf mood falls first time since 2009 -poll\b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 01:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Manufacturing, service-sector sentiment seen deteriorating\par \par \uc2 * Confidence up from June, pointing to rise in BOJ tankan\par \par \uc2 * Tankan recovery may slow, boding ill for BOJ data trend\par \par \uc2 * As tankan loses steam, pressure could rise for more easing (Adds analyst quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Izumi Nakagawa\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - Japanese manufacturing confidence worsened in September from the previous month for the first time in nearly a year as companies struggle with a persistent yen rise that threatens a fragile, export-reliant economic recovery, a Reuters poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Compared with three months ago, however, manufacturers' mood was moderately better, pointing to continued improvement in the Bank of Japan's next quarterly tankan corporate sentiment survey, which is highly correlated with the Reuters monthly poll.\par \par \uc2 The pace of quarterly gains in the Reuters Tankan has nevertheless slowed somewhat from earlier this year and is seen declining further in the next three months, underscoring companies' wariness over the yen and slowing global growth.\par \par \uc2 That bodes ill for the overall trend in the BOJ's key survey, which is due out on Sept. 29. Any bleak readings could add to pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for further easing to cope with the plight of companies facing yen gains and deflation.\par \par \uc2 Japan stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday for the first time in six years, selling yen to stem a rise that is threatening a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ tankan will likely show manufacturing sentiment improving further but only slightly given that companies are growing more cautious about the outlook after the Reuters poll was taken," said Yoshimasa Maruyama, an economist at \uc2 Itochu Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Further monetary easing by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will depend on the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they work in tandem. The greater the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the greater the chance of further BOJ easing," he said.\par \par \uc2 SENTIMENT DETERIORATING\par \par \uc2 The monthly poll, which has a 95 percent correlation with the BOJ's tankan survey, showed manufacturers' sentiment index fell 5 points from August to plus 17, down for the first time since October 2009. Service-sector sentiment improved 6 points to minus 4 but has stayed in negative territory since June 2008.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment is seen deteriorating further to plus 2 in manufacturing and minus 9 in the service sector in the three months to December, according to the Aug. 27-Sept. 13 poll of 400 big firms, of which 229 responded.\par \par \uc2 "Our profits deteriorate as the yen rises," one auto firm said. "While the market remains sluggish due to oversupply and price declines, we're concerned about rises in materials costs."\par \par \uc2 Another firm complained about a lack of effective government steps to back its business even as subsidies on fuel-efficient vehicles expire this month.\par \par \uc2 Chemicals and autos/transport equipment are among those sectors which have suffered badly.\par \par \uc2 Companies complained about a profit squeeze as they struggle to pass on costs to customers amid weak demand and deflation.\par \par \uc2 The government unveiled a package of measures to support the economy last week but analysts doubt it could have much impact, as there is little room for sizeable spending with a public debt twice the size of Japan's $5 trillion economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy grew a revised 0.4 percent in the second quarter, but analysts expect it to hit a soft patch later this year as a boost from government stimulus fades and growth slows in key export destinations such as the United States and China.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ eased policy further at an emergency meeting on Aug. 30, bowing to government pressure for a policy response after the yen's sharp rises. It will hold its next rate review on Oct. 4-5.\par \par \uc2 The indexes in the Reuters Tankan are derived by subtracting the percentage of pessimistic respondents from optimistic ones. A negative figure means pessimists outnumber optimists. (Writing by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/TANKAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f001kr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
