\par\par \b \uc2 STOCKS NEWS ASIA-Shares to rise cautiously as yen weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 00:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON, Sept 16 (Reuters) - Asian stocks will probably rise on Thursday, helped by gains in the U.S. and a steadily falling yen <JPY=> in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The main Wall Street indexes were up as much as 0.5 percent, with the market seen rangebound as the S&P 500 <.SPX> could not climb above a key technical level, which might spark further buying.\par \par \uc2 The market was pressured early by soft data showing New York state business conditions slipped to the lowest level in more than a year, and industrial output rose at a slower rate in August. [ID:nN15145257].\par \par \uc2 But a positive forecast from Dow <.DJI> component \uc2 Kraft Foods Inc\uc2  helped to turn around sentiment. See [ID:nN15130871]\par \par \uc2 Analysts said Wall Street's recent strong run helped investors look through the bad news to the gradually improving economic picture.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks listed on Wall Street <.BKAS> rose 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 British <.FTSE> and European <.FTEU3> shares dipped 0.2 percent, as markets were cautious on the U.S. data and a fall in crude oil <CLc1> prices dragged energy stocks lower.\par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> fell 3 percent against the U.S. dollar in the wake of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although the market was unconvinced this would be an effective long term solution. For more see [USD/]\par \par \uc2 Japanese markets are seen building on Wednesday's 2.3 percent rise, as exporters were cheered by the fading yen. Nikkei futures traded in Chicago <2NKc1> 180 points above the last closing level in Osaka <JNIc1>.\par \par \uc2 Australian shares are also seen marginally higher, with share index futures <YAPcm1> up 11 points to 4,668, a 6.5 point premium to the close of the underlying S&P/ASX 200 index <.AXJO>.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  HEADLINES: > Wall St advances but remains range-bound      \par   [nN15193577] > \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's exporters rise on yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     \par \par  [nN15160918] > \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken yen; more to come   \par  [nTOE68E02W] > US industry, lawmakers urge action on China's\par yuan[nN15135709] > US industrial output growth slows;import\par prices up[nN15145257] > EU claims victory in WTO case versus\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2        [nLDE68E1M6] > Soros warns on gold rally, says\par nothing safe      [nN15153856] > Obama looks to jump-start Wall\par St,/health reforms [nN15152410] > NZ cenbank holds rate, future\par rises gradual       [nSGE68E044] > \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO: no interest in\par Alibaba stake sale      [nN15165387] > EU unveils clampdown on\par financial speculators     [nLDE68E18S]     --\par  KEY INDEXES                                      pct change\par  Bank of New York Asia ADR index <.BKAS>            +0.36\par  Dow Jones Industrial Average <.DJI>                +0.44\par  Nasdaq Composite Index <.IXIC>                     +0.50\par  Nikkei futures in Chicago <2NKc1>                  9,650\par  Nikkei futures in Osaka <JNIc1>                    9,470\par  --\par  TOP ASIA EVENTS:\par  For more Asian company earnings, see ASIA/EQTY\par  JAPAN     - Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa gives\par              speeches to two conferences\par            - Reuters tankan for September. [JPRTAN=ECI]\par       - Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association\par    Chairman Toshiyuki Shiga news confe