\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Japan yen tactic muddles bid to drive yuan up\b0\par\par\uc2 838 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 00:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Japan's yen move complicates US, European push on yuan\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say Japan move risks beggar-thy-neighbor rush\par \par \uc2 * U.S. lawmaker says China, Japan using 'predatory' policy\par \par \uc2 * Obama administration mute on wisdom of Tokyo's action\par \par \uc2 By Glenn Somerville\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 15 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to single-handedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to drive down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value complicates a U.S. and European bid to persuade export-powerhouse China to let its yuan appreciate.\par \par \uc2 Amid growing global pressure for Beijing to let its yuan rise in value to help rebalance uneven global trade, analysts warn that Japan's unilateral action risks spurring an era of beggar-thy-neighbor policies as countries try to devalue their way to prosperity.\par \par \uc2 Already on Wednesday, Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was starting to buy at least $20 million daily to slow the rise in its peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega said he was "watching the game" and wouldn't stand by if others weakened their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Brazil's exporting expense.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, business leaders are urging policymakers to keep the baht's value from rising excessively so that its industrial sector doesn't suffer, and analysts see a risk that Thailand and other Asian nations may follow \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's path.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not unexpected. The yen had hit a 15-year high that was a threat to Japan's recovery, and officials in Tokyo had been threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for weeks.\par \par \uc2 U.S. lawmakers, embarking on the first of two days of hearings into China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, let their anger show on Wednesday at the two Asian trade giants while venting frustration at the difficulty in shrinking trade imbalances.\par \par \uc2 "China is not the only country with a predatory exchange rate policy," said U.S. House of Representatives' Ways and Means Committee Chairman Sander Levin, describing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "a deeply disturbing development."\par \par \uc2 "What's happening is that China's actions have affected Japan, and now Japan's actions affect us," he said.\par \par \uc2 Displeasure at Tokyo on Capitol Hill was in marked contrast with the studied quiet from the Obama administration and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House and U.S. Treasury declined comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as did the Federal Reserve, possibly a sign that Japanese officials had sought their silence during the runup to the overnight bout of dollar buying.\par \par \uc2 The silence was nonetheless telling, effectively highlighting the conundrum that U.S. officials face.\par \par \uc2 As Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago, put it: "How can the Japanese get a pass to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the Chinese are being criticized for essentially the same activity?"\par \par \uc2 NOT THE PROPER ROUTE\par \par \uc2 The closest that U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner came to publicly trying to dissuade Japan from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action came last week when he was asked during a television interview if the United States would back \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "My view is that they should be focusing, as we are, on how to make sure they are reinforcing recovery," he said. "That would be good for us, good for Japan's trading partners."\par \par \uc2 The timing of Tokyo's action, ahead of gatherings of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank in Washington early in October and Group of 20 meetings in Korea that conclude with a leaders summit in early November, added to a sense of discomfort.\par \par \uc2 Those bodies -- and the smaller Group of Seven that will meet informally on the sidelines of the IMF meeting in Washington -- are the key forums that focus on the need for market-influenced exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 When the G7 has sanctioned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, it has generally been on a coordinated basis as opposed to unilateral actions, such as Japan's, that might trigger competitive devaluations.\par \par \uc2 "Unilateral actions are not the appropriate way to deal with global imbalances," Eurogroup Chairman Jean-Claude Juncker said on Wednesday when asked about Japan's yen selling.\par \par \uc2 Desmond Lachman, a fellow at the American Enterprise Institute in Washington, said Japanese authorities were rightly concerned at the huge appreciation the yen has seen in the past year as its popularity as a 'safe haven' \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soared.\par \par \uc2 The yen's rise was in part a play by global investors on the low level of interest rates in the United States and the possibility that the United States. and Europe might have to ease monetary policy further if recovery falters.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's policysetting committee meets next Tuesday. While it is not expected to announce new easing measures, it will debate what can and should be done to try to speed up a wan recovery that is not creating jobs at a pace sufficient to lower unemployment. (additional reporting by Paul Eckert, Doug Palmer and Emily Kaiser)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|Z|UKI|J  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
