\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  COLUMN- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  faces difficult war on yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 11:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 -- John Noonan is head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at IFR Markets and Rick Lloyd is a Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst. The views expressed are their own --\par \par \uc2 By John Noonan and Rick Lloyd\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/SINGAPORE, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Japan appears to have scored an early victory with Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen's rise. But if previous bouts of unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are anything to go by, today's success is only the start of what will be a difficult war.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollar/yen <JPY=> after it hit a 15-year low of 82.87 early in the Asian session can be deemed a success, lifting USD/JPY above the psychologically important 85.00 level by the end of the Asian day as a very short market was forced to cover.\par \par \uc2 The effort to boost the USD/JPY through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone is unlikely to succeed and a test of 80.00 at some stage is probable. But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coupled with an aggressive increase in quantitative easing from the BOJ, and the USD/JPY could move back above 90.00.\par \par \uc2 Scepticism is running high that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with anything like the "awesome" amounts, as former Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan dubbed them, seen in the three-month, 15 trillion yen ($180 billion) spree in early 2004.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention that daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  turnover is twice as big as it was six years ago at $4 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Once the weak USD/JPY shorts are stopped out or the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes a break from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market will commence selling dollar/yen -- and that wave of selling will not only come from the speculative community.\par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters have been selling anaemic rallies in the USD/JPY since it broke below 85.00 a few weeks ago and they are likely to be aggressive sellers on rallies above the 85.00 level, trying to finish hedging before the end of the quarter and first half of the fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Japanese life insurers will also be keen to take advantage of any spikes in USD/JPY as they approach the fiscal half year end, as will speculative retail accounts.\par \par \uc2 Market players who were around back in 2003 will remember the failure of the MOF to hold USD/JPY up that year, and past unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts show that the market and authorities will end up in a game of cat and mouse.\par \par \uc2 The market will look to sell extended rallies above 85.00 and take profit when the MOF/BOJ buys below 83.00.\par \par \uc2 Past unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that attempted to draw a line in the sand have typically resulted in the market getting rewarded if it is patient enough: just look at EUR/CHF near record lows despite the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's big \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The only chance the Japanese authorities have in putting an end to medium-term USD/JPY weakness and preventing a move below 80.00 is to couple the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts with a significant ramp up in quantitative easing from the BOJ -- effectively monetizing debt.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has publicly stated it is against taking further quantitative easing steps. Last week, BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa noted that past efforts to employ "quantitative easing" to boost the economy and increase price pressures had "little effect".\par \par \uc2 The independent BOJ does not want to be seen supporting government fiscal spending by buying government debt beyond what it already purchases in its "rinban" \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some analysts see the major Japanese political parties finding a consensus that will effectively end the BOJ's independence, including one proposal for a law forcing the BOJ to adopt an inflation target between 2 percent and 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Jesper Koll, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's Japan head of equity research, wrote in a recent note that some lawmakers are discussing forcing the BOJ to step up direct debt monetization to boost base-money growth by adding up to 50 trillion yen in JGB purchases.\par \par \uc2 Naoto Kan was victorious in retaining his role as prime minister and head of the ruling Democratic Party of Japan. But a day later, the government's actions are starting to look like those of Ichiro Ozawa, his opponent who had promised aggressive measures to defeat deflation -- including unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Editing by Eric Burroughs and Jonathan Thatcher)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (COLUMN)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|J|NAT|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f000ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
