\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-BOJ FOCUS-BOJ won't join yen fight now but may ease in Oct\b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (Repeats story that first ran late on Wednesday)\par \par \uc2  * BOJ in no mood to rush after last month's emergency meeting\par \par \uc2  * May move sooner if \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails, pressures mount\par \par \uc2  * Ready to act Oct 4-5 if U.S. slowdown, yen still a threat\par \par \uc2  By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 15 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no plan to support government yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an immediate easing of monetary policy, sources said, but it will be prepared to act in early October if the fragile economic recovery remains under threat.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an emergency meeting late in August, so sees no need for another one so soon. However, it could change its mind if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to check the rise in the yen and triggers strong political pressure for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.\par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped 2 yen on Wednesday from a 15-year low after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years.\par \par \uc2  Markets have long speculated about potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might then respond since policymakers have been concerned for months that the yen's rise was hampering Japan's recovery from the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2  "There is absolutely no plan for an emergency meeting, at least now," a source familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking said.\par \par \uc2  "But as the governor has said, we will act in a timely manner when necessary. If there is enough evidence of downside risks for the economy, there is no reason to wait," the source said.\par \par \uc2  A further loosening of policy on Oct. 4-5 cannot be ruled out, he said. Another source expressed a similar view. Both declined to be identified due to the sensitivity of the matter.\par \par \uc2  BUYING GOVERNMENT BONDS FAVOURED\par \par \uc2  The BOJ cut its policy rate to just 0.1 percent during the crisis and in three moves dating back to December 2009 has set up a loans scheme for banks, which many analysts have said was primarily aimed at keeping yen gains in check.\par \par \uc2  In June, it set up a separate loan scheme for banks aimed at boosting lending to firms with growth potential. [ID:nTOE65E036]\par \par \uc2  Now the BOJ is lining up its options for easing policy again.\par \par \uc2  An increase in government bond buying is emerging as a more likely option than other steps, such as expanding the cheap fund-supply tool yet again, sources said.\par \par \uc2  But there is no consensus yet within the BOJ on what action should be taken, if need be.\par \par \uc2  Increasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases of government bonds is a particularly sensitive issue now because Japan has a public debt twice the size of its $5 trillion economy, leaving the government with little room for fiscal expansion.\par \par \uc2  Indeed, some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, including Governor Masaaki Shirakawa, have opposed buying more government bonds in case the move is interpreted by markets as a willingness by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to print money to fund public debt.\par \par \uc2  Other possibilities being prepared for discussion include the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying private-sector assets from banks, or increasing the size of the BOJ's loan programme for growth industries.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ is due to hold two policy meetings in October. At the second meeting on Oct. 28, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will issue a twice-yearly report that is widely expected to revise down its long-term growth forecasts and project a delay in Japan's exit from deflation.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ has kept interest rates near zero and currently buys 21.6 trillion yen ($260.2 billion) in long-term government bonds per year from the market.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ can increase its government bond buying by around 200 billion yen per month.\par \par \uc2  A bigger amount would mean the BOJ would have to review its self-imposed rule of capping government bond holdings to the amount of bank notes in circulation, something sources say it is not ready to do just yet.  (Editing by Neil Fullick)  ($1=83.00 Yen)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (ANALYSIS, REPEAT)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100915e69f000hv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
