\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Higher; Data Mixed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 00:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Malaysian markets are closed Thursday for Malaysia Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10572.73  +46.24  +0.44% \par Nasdaq     2301.32  +11.55  +0.50% \par S&P 500    1125.07   +3.97  +0.35% \par FTSE 100   5555.56  -11.85  -0.21% \par Xetra DAX  6261.87  -13.54  -0.22% \par  \par 2Y Tsy 99 25/32  +1/32 0.488% -1.1BP \par 5Y Tsy  99 2/32 flat   1.447% flat \par 10Y Tsy 99 5/32 -11/32 2.721% +4.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.72-76     +3.18% \par Range         85.77-82.88 \par EUR/USD       1.3008-11    +0.10% \par Range         1.3036-1.2956 \par AUD/USD       0.9380-81    -0.27% \par Range         0.9428-0.9344 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    11.73-11.98% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    10.78-11.18% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    11.85-12.15% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,268.70 -$3.00 \par Comex Gold Range    $1264.50-$1,273.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $76.02   -$0.78 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rose Wednesday as encouraging forecasts from \uc2 Kraft Foods\uc2  and McKesson boosted investors' expectations of how the corporate sector is faring in the weak economy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 46.24, or 0.44%, to 10572.73, its highest close since Aug. 10 and its ninth gain in the past 11 sessions. Kraft was among the measure's best performers, up 54 cents, or 1.7%, to 31.59. The company, which recently bought confectioner Cadbury, said it expects sales from its chocolate business to jump to $10 billion by 2013, driven by developing markets and added revenue growth from the acquisition. Kraft executives, speaking at a New York analyst meeting, also forecasted growth in its biscuits business and its gum-and-candy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite rose 11.55, or 0.50%, to 2301.32, its sixth-straight session in the black.\par \par \uc2 The Standard & Poor's 500 index added 3.97, or 0.35%, to 1125.07. The measure's gains were led by the health-care sector, which was boosted by a jump in McKesson of 3.24, or 5.4%, to 62.98. The pharmaceutical supplier told investors at a conference that it expects to hit fiscal-year guidance despite slowing health-care volumes.\par \par \uc2 "Companies are indicating that business continues to be as strong as they expected going into the third quarter," said Tim Evnin, partner and equity portfolio manager at Evercore Wealth Management. "I would be hesitant to say all the clouds have cleared, but the sentiment appears to be better among [corporate] management."\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's activity followed a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, marking the first time Japanese authorities publicly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2004. The move sent the dollar sharply higher against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "Obviously, the yen has been on a tear and they really want to do something about that," Evnin said. Still, he noted that market participants are skeptical over how much of a long-term impact the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can ultimately have.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended at their lowest level in two weeks Wednesday as banks fell amid lingering concerns over the prospect of an interest rate hike by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and further efforts by the country's banking regulator to strengthen lenders' balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely stay in a tight range between 2650 and 2750 this week as investors await policy clues from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 "This month may be the turning point for China's rising inflation, but investors still haven't ruled out the possibility of an interest rate hike," said Zhang Gang, an analyst at Central China Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks surged Wednesday, with the Nikkei Stock Average posting its biggest gain in more than five weeks as major exporters gained after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time in more than six years to halt the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 "Since the impression had been that (Prime Minister Naoto) Kan had been late in taking measures to address the stronger yen, the timing of this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lead to a significant boost in investor sentiment," said \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  equity strategist Fumiyuki Takahashi, adding that the Nikkei index may rise to 10,000 by year-end.\par \par \uc2 While investors welcomed a speedy response to the strong yen from Kan following his win Tuesday in the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's leadership election against veteran lawmaker Ichiro Ozawa, analysts cautioned that the stock gains may be short-lived unless the government takes other economic measures to combat deflation.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) is like dressing a flesh wound," said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. "This is not a fundamental solution."\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices settled lower in U.S. trading Wednesday, held down by a stronger dollar and signs of a setback in U.S. manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures pulled back Wednesday as market participants sold holdings to cash in on the metal's record highs and demand declined on a lack of market moving news.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed mostly lower Wednesday, weighed down by weaker-than-expected U.S. data and speculation that China will step up measures to cool its property market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen plummeted against the dollar Wednesday after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in more than six years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar gained more than 3% against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after having dropped Tuesday to its lowest level in 15 years against the yen. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again Thursday in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets if necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerns over the pace of the U.S. recovery have recently sent investors flocking to the perceived safety of the yen, especially as investors speculate whether the Federal Reserve could implement another round of asset purchases to kick-start a moribund economy, which would likely weigh further on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might temporarily push the dollar higher against the yen, but is unlikely to have a longer-term effect, said Vassili Serebriakov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The yen's [recent] moves are really mostly to do with what's happening in the U.S.," Serebriakov said of the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength. Until the U.S. notches a more solid recovery, "the yen's medium-term trend will be driven by the U.S. economic and monetary-policy developments," he said, questioning the lasting effectiveness of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the dollar dropped Tuesday to the 15-year low of Y82.87, the initial barrage of yen-selling orders hit the market early in the Asian day; it was followed by bursts that pushed the dollar above Y85.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight announced the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  about 20 minutes after the first sales hit the market. The massive selling began with an initial wave of Y200 billion-Y300 billion, followed by continual bursts to total an estimated Y1 trillion through the Asian trading day, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The authorities then continued selling yen into European trading and were prepared to extend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  into the U.S. session if needed, a Japanese government official said. The official said the Finance Ministry's action was partly aimed at countering speculative yen buying.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the dollar was at Y85.63, according to EBS via CQG, slightly off its Y85.78 high. The dollar remained stuck around Y85.60 through most of the North American session, with no spikes higher during New York trading that would indicate direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They could be \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via stealth" measures, but "we're not seeing anything on the charts," or in the bank's own flows, during North American trading, said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Stamford, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, meanwhile, ticked down only slightly against the dollar, trading at $1.3012 from $1.3020 late Tuesday. The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also was sharply higher against the yen, at Y111.36 from Y108.20. The U.K. pound was at $1.5623 from $1.5563. The dollar was at CHF1.0035 from CHF0.9950.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was up at 81.453 from 81.074.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided the yuan higher against the U.S. dollar for the fourth consecutive trading session Wednesday. Shortly after the start of trading, the yuan was at 6.7330 to the dollar, its strongest level since it began trading in 1994. At that level, the yuan was up 1.4% against the dollar since June 19, when China dropped the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 23-month-long de facto peg to the U.S. unit and pledged to make the yuan more flexible and market oriented. The dollar ended at CNY6.7422 Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Russian ruble also fell to a multi-month low against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar-euro \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, despite relatively steady oil prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in six years to rein in the yen's gain Wednesday boosted demand for short-dated Treasurys while weakening long-dated securities for the first time this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year and 30-year maturities bore the brunt of the selling in a choppy trading session as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surprising \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market spurred many investors to shift money into short-dated notes that are believed to be Japan's favored places to park its dollar proceeds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Many investors are avoiding the long end of the Treasury market because the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will bring money into the front end of the bond market," said Tom di Galoma, head of U.S. rates trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guggenheim Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "Treasurys inside three years should benefit the most from the flows."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1951 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was down 11/32 in price and its yield was up to 2.721%. The 30-year bond was down 1 7/32 to yield 3.869%. The three-year note was up 1/32 to yield 0.766% while the two-year note was up 1/32 to yield 0.488%. Bond yields move inversely to prices. [ 15-09-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such trades lifted demand for Treasurys maturing in seven years or less in morning trade, but only the two-year and three-year notes held some gains in the afternoon session as new supply looms. The Treasury Department is scheduled to release the sizes for next week's Treasury auctions in two-year, five-year and seven-year maturities Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-dated maturities were the big losers as reduced speculation about large-scale purchases of Treasurys by the Federal Reserve also weighed, as did hedge selling from companies issuing new corporate debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Wednesday as comments by PBOC adviser Li Daokui that raising deposit interest rates would benefit China's financial stability renewed concerns over possible interest rate hikes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Concerns over possible tightening measures have hurt the bond market sentiment, spurring heavy selling this afternoon," a Shanghai-based trader with a local brokerage said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-term support for the Shanghai exchange government bond index ends is tipped at 126.75.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lead Japanese government bond futures ended sharply higher Wednesday, as the victory of fiscally prudent Naoto Kan in a ruling party election helped ease oversupply concerns. The upside was somewhat limited, however, by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to sell yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the DPJ presidential election over and the bond market's fiscal concerns alleviated, players are willing to take advantage of the "excessive" JGB price falls that took place ahead of Tuesday's election, said Maki Shimizu, senior strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Players initially sold off JGBs on the MOF's action. But demand remains strong after Kan's win, and with no more long-term JGB auctions planned this month, said Nobuto Yamazaki, executive fund manager at \f28 \uc2 \f2 DLIBJ Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the market will closely examine the effects of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the 10-year cash JGB yield is expected to decline to around 1.0% in the near term, Yamazaki said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Shimizu also expects any impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be limited on JGBs, and the overall bond market trend to remain solid in the latter half of this month. She said the MOF's move alone won't likely reverse the yen's broad upward momentum.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Thursday, Hong Kong's jobless rate is likely to have held steady at 4.3% in the June-August period, according to a median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Industrial Output Cools; Import Prices Rise \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Launches Y1T \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Yen Tumbles \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  In NY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market - Sources \par MOF: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  To Continue Yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  On Thurs If Necessary-Kyodo \par =WSJ:\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Yen Move Is Quick Fix That Ignores Deeper Problems \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Online Banking Site Inaccessible \par Despite New Rules, Money Funds Add Risk \par Geithner Defends White House Tax Plans \par Door Open On Tax Cuts For Wealthy \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Not Looking To Sell Alibaba Stake \par Kraft Sees Chocolate Business Sales Of $10B In 2013 \par US Mortgage-refinance Activity Slumps \par Reserve Bank Of NZ Keeps Cash Rate Unch At 3.00% \par =WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Yahoo\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO Bartz: Company Not Looking To Sell Alibaba Stake \par =WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chevron\f28 \uc2 \f2 : Near Natural Gas Deal With CNPC \par WSJ: Toyota's Scion Brand Counting On Launch Of 2 New Models In Next 7 Mos \par US Files 2 WTO Cases Vs China On Steel, Electronic Payments \par =Fonterra: Whole-Milk Powder Prices Rise 1.4% In Auction \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2350  Japan        Jul Tertiary Industry Index \par 0100  Australia    Sep Consumer Inflationa