\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Closes Weaker After Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Murphy   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 21:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed weaker against the U.S. dollar Wednesday after the Japanese government shook up international markets with an aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL1.7258 to the dollar, weaker against the Tuesday close of BRL1.7055.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government Wednesday sold yen into the market in an effort to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent appreciation. The move led to a strengthening of the U.S. dollar against the yen and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro and the real.\par \par \uc2 "The Japanese move has given pause to everyone in the market here in Brazil," said a Sao Paulo-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "The strength of the U.S. dollar against the yen and the euro Wednesday has helped prevent a strengthening of the real beyond the BRL1.70 barrier."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has the same worries as Japan's. The continued strengthening of the Brazilian real in recent days could hurt exports. The real posted six winning sessions in a row through Tuesday. A week ago, the real was trading at BRL1.73 to the dollar, a month ago at BRL1.77.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the solution to the problem of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be the same for Brazil as for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and could come in the form of increased government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, in comments Wednesday afternoon, Brazilian Finance Minister \uc2 Guido Mantega\uc2  signaled that the government was ready to soak up "excess" dollar inflows through purchases from the market.\par \par \uc2 On cue, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday bought dollars from the market in two separate snap auctions.\par \par \uc2 But the underlying problem of heavy dollar inflows continues, according to trader Paulo Fujisaki of Sao Paulo's Socopa brokerage. "There have been heavy inflows in recent days from overseas bond placements and there will continue to be heavy inflows in the coming days because of the \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  share offer," said Fujisaki.\par \par \uc2 Government-controlled oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  (PBR, PETR4.BR) is organizing a massive share offer for later in September. The offer is sparking lively interest among foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Some of that foreign investor money may have already entered the country. According to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures released Wednesday afternoon, net investment inflows into Brazil in the first 10 days of September amounted to $2.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Fujisaki, however, said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was likely to return to a more orderly routine following the \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  share offer. He predicted a range of BRL1.70 to BRL1.80 for the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was volatile Wednesday, credit markets remained subdued, with little change in the panorama for either growth or inflation. Brazil's economy is likely to expand by at least 6% this year with inflation under 5%.\par \par \uc2 On the Brazilian Futures Exchange, interest rate futures contracts were mixed in range-bound trading. The contracts reflect investor expectations for annualized interest rates at future dates.\par \par \uc2 Among the most actively traded interest rate futures contracts Wednesday, that of January 2012 closed at 11.33%, up from 11.32% Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 -By Tom Murphy, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3544-7090; brazil@dowjones.com [ 15-09-10 1933GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f001cg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
