\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS: European Stocks Dip; Investors Cautious\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrea Tryphonides and Ishaq Siddiqi   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 13:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--European stocks edged lower Wednesday as investors adopted a cautious stance while digesting a raft of economic data releases and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By 1135 GMT, the Stoxx Europe 600 index was 0.3% lower at 265.60, while London's FTSE 100 index fell 0.2% to 5556.80. In Paris, the CAC-40 index was 0.4% lower at 3759.19, and Frankfurt's DAX index declined 0.3% to 6256.97.\par \par \uc2 In terms of U.S. equity futures, the December Dow Jones Industrial Average futures contract was down 0.2% at 10,440.0 and S&P 500 futures contract was 0.3% lower at 1112.8.\par \par \uc2 In the session's economic news, euro-zone HICP inflation eased to 1.6% in August from 1.7% in July. Economists agreed that underlying inflationary pressures remain subdued in the euro area.\par \par \uc2 In London, U.K. claimant count unemployment rose in August for the first time since January this year, hinting at tough times ahead once government spending cuts begin to bite in earnest.\par \par \uc2 Also, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  downgraded the technology sector as part of its European equity strategy, and this was clearly weighing on the sector. The Stoxx 600 technology index was last seen down 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Also, healthcare stocks dropped on news that U.S. regulators have postponed a key decision on \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 's Brilinta drug. Shares in \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  fell 1.3% to 3340.0 pence in London, and shares in peer \uc2 Sanofi-Aventis\uc2  fell 0.6% to EUR48.90.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, the main news came from Japan, with Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda confirming selling of the yen and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued even as the dollar climbed above Y84, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently sold yen in $10 million lots.\par \par \uc2 "The deep pockets of the Japanese authorities suggest that they may have some success in slowing the pace of the yen's appreciation," said \uc2 Rabobank International\uc2 . "However, in the past authorities have tended only to be successful in turning around the direction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair if support comes from sea-change in fundamental drivers."\par \par \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  noted the recent better tone of U.S. data may help reverse demand for safe-haven assets such as the yen. However, U.S. yields are currently very low prompted by another round of speculation Tuesday that the Federal Reserve may be positioning itself for another round of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar will struggle to win back significant ground with U.S. treasury yields at current levels, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Japanese stocks posted strong gains, as exporters breathed a sigh of relief after the yen fell sharply on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities, the first in six years.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei Stock Average rose 2.3%, as local traders estimated that the authorities' yen sales were worth around Y200 billion to Y300 billion in the early minutes of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before climbing to an estimated Y1 trillion. This sent the dollar surging above the Y85 level, compared with an earlier low of Y82.87. The euro tagged along and rose past Y110 from Y107.74 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Other Asian stock markets were mixed as the varying effects of Japan's move were digested across the region. South Korea's Kospi Composite was up 0.5% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was up 0.1%, while the Shanghai Composite Index was off 1.3%.\par \par \uc2 In foreign exchanges, at 1145 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y85.27, up from Y83.04 in New York late Tuesday, while the euro was at $1.2974, down from $1.2998, but at Y110.62, up from Y107.91.\par \par \uc2 Among other asset classes, at 1145 GMT spot gold was quoted at $1269.20 a troy ounce, up $1 from late New York trading. Meanwhile, the October Nymex crude oil futures contract was down $1.08 at $75.72 per barrel. In the bond markets, the December bund futures contract was down 0.22 at 130.29.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. import price index is at 1230 GMT, along with the Empire state manufacturing index, while August industrial production is due at 1315 GMT.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrea Tryphonides, Dow Jones Newswires; +44-20-7842-9281; andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com [ 15-09-10 1150GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f000zb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
