\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOJ To Let Yen From  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Slosh Around Market\b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 12:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Combines previous stories, recasts, adds analysis)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Megumi Fujikawa and Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to augment the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by leaving at least some of the massive amounts of yen in the market, where it could further depress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ sees "no need to absorb" the extra yen from the government's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years as that could blunt the effectiveness of the Japanese government's efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 BOJ policy board member Tadao Noda, speaking to reporters in Shimonoseki in southern Japan, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would use the yen sold by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide "ample liquidity" to the Tokyo money market.\par \par \uc2 Leaving the yen in the banking system is an unorthodox attempt to turbo-charge the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Central banks usually "sterilize" \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , draining the extra liquidity to keep overall cash in line with demand.\par \par \uc2 The extra supply of yen in the market theoretically should reduce its price. Analysts also say the effect of such an announcement itself can change market psychology.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ "aims to increase the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via money-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 --in cooperation with the Finance Ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," the person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinking told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The government expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, other people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to combat the "severe economic conditions" caused by the yen's surge, which hurts Japan's export-reliant economy and deepens the country's deflation.\par \par \uc2 The ministry dumped some Y1 trillion on the market, buying the dollar aggressively all day from the morning's 15-year low of Y82.87 all the way above Y85, traders said.\par \par \uc2 That compares to an average one-day total of Y328 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  going back to 1998, according to \uc2 Westpac\uc2  analysts. This marks \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, when the Finance Ministry ended a 15-month campaign in which it sold a record Y35 trillion in total.\par \par \uc2 While Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is huge, it's a drop in the liquidity ocean of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where $4 trillion changes hands a day, meaning the government has launched what may be another titanic struggle.\par \par \uc2 That's where the BOJ comes in. Although the Finance Ministry controls \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ decides how to handle the money afterward.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from politicians to ease policy further but with interest rates near zero, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be agreeing to leave the extra yen in the market in what is essentially a "quantitative easing," or flooding of the banking system with cash to bolster lending and combat deflation.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan strongly expects that the action taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will contribute to a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rate formation," BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said in a statement after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Still, the BOJ's Noda told reporters at a news conference that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "won't affect BOJ's monetary policy," saying that what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does next will be determined based on economic and price conditions.\par \par \uc2 It is "important" if the BOJ indeed refrains from sterilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Westpac\uc2  analysts said in a note to investors.\par \par \uc2 "If the BOJ wants to arrest yen strength and address deflation, it needs to expand its balance sheet and print more yen," they said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had previously shown little evidence of bloating its balance sheet to weaken the yen, but if it leaves the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  said it would finally see a reason to sell the yen against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Although unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered unorthodox, there is some evidence that the BOJ has been here before.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record 2003-2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign, "about 60% of the yen funds supplied to the market by yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were immediately offset by the BOJ's monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a professor and a BOJ official wrote in a 2007 paper.\par \par \uc2 "The remaining 40% were not offset and remained in the market for some time," said Tsutomu Watanabe of Hitotsubashi University and Tomoyoshi Yabu of the BOJ. "This is in contrast with the preceding period, when almost 100% were offset."\par \par \uc2 -By Megumi Fujikawa and Takashi Nakamichi; Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Tomoyuki Tachikawa in Shimonoseki continued to this article.) [ 15-09-10 1009GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f000uk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
