\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Slightly Weaker Late After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 367 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 09:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.3371   +0.0007 \par    Overnight rate        0.13%    unchanged \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.49%    unchanged \par    10-year bond yield:   2.16%    +3 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  0.95%    +2 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   2.48%    +4 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   153 bps  +2 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was marginally weaker in late Asian trade Wednesday, retreating from the record high it struck in offshore trade after the Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to defend a sliding U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The greenback fell sharply against the euro and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore trade as speculation the Federal Reserve may need to do more to support the U.S. economy weighed on the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Ministry, operating through the Bank of Japan, sold yen aggressively from Wednesday morning for the first time since March 2004, driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply higher against the yen from 15-year lows and against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's thinking Wednesday said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gauging the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is expected to help other Asian authorities cope with their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the U.S. dollar falls back to around S$1.3300 from current levels but is watching the market for now, the person told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 However, a trader at a local bank said the U.S. dollar is unlikely fall back toward S$1.3300 in the next few days with the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia still high.\par \par \uc2 "There's always the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS and other central banks as well because of Japan's move," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 Resistance for the greenback is likely around S$1.3400.\par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond yields were higher at the longer end as firmer equities markets pushed up yields.\par \par \uc2 -By Sam Holmes, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4157; samuel.holmes@dowjones.com [ 15-09-10 0752GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f000mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
