\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Asian Shares Mixed; Tokyo Surges On Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 07:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (=UPDATE: Asian Shares Mixed; Tokyo Surges On Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," at 0419 GMT, misstated in the fourth paragraph the actions of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 By Colin Ng and Matthew Allen\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stocks were mixed Wednesday while the Tokyo market staged a stunning turnaround after the yen fell sharply due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities.\par \par \uc2 Local traders estimated that authorities' yen sales were worth around Y200 billion to Y300 billion in the early minutes of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , their first in six years.\par \par \uc2 That sent the greenback surging toward the Y85 level, compared with an earlier low of Y82.87. The euro tagged along and rose past Y110 from Y107.74 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda confirmed that the government was selling the yen and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued even as the dollar climbed above Y84, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently sold yen in $10 million lots.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was up 2.7% at 9550.34, climbing sharply into positive territory from an intraday low of 9199.08, after the government's foray into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 But despite the apparent success of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in providing a shot in the arm for the stock market, analysts were not confident the gains would last.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like putting a band aid on a person, in this case a country bleeding from deflation...this (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not a fundamental solution," said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro.\par \par \uc2 He added that "the timing (of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) came as a surprise for market players who had been betting on yen to rise and stocks to fall" after Prime Minister Naoto Kan Tuesday beat back political heavyweight Ichiro Ozawa's challenge for the leadership of the ruling Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That scenario had been playing out prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, as Ozawa had publicly pledged market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen if he was chosen for the top job, while Kan was seen as less likely to make such a move.\par \par \uc2 Gains were broad based with 32 out of the 33 Topix subindexes trading higher. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  was up 3.5%, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  gained 3.5% and \uc2 Sony\uc2  was up 2.4% reversing earlier losses.\par \par \uc2 Other markets were mixed as the varying effects of Japan's move were digested across the region. Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was up 0.5%, South Korea's Kospi Composite was down 0.1%. The Shanghai Composite Index was off 0.7%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index was flat and India's Sensex was up 0.6%. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up eleven points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Australia's headline share index set a fresh four-month high as Japan's move on the yen was fueling speculation of renewed investment in riskier assets, including Australian equities.\par \par \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  was up 1.8% on resumption of trade after it dropped its bid for AXA Asia Pacific. Other major banks were up 0.2% to 0.8%. \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  was flat while BHP Billiton was up 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Gold mining stocks were higher in several markets with \uc2 Newcrest Mining\uc2  up 1.2%, helped by a record high for the yellow metal. Japan's \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  rose 1.1%. In Hong Kong, Zijin Mining was up 1.7% and Zhaojin Mining gained 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold hit a record around $1,275 a troy ounce Tuesday and was recently down $1.10 cents from late New York levels at $1,267.60.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Korea had turned lower as the fall in the yen weighed on technology names and crimped automobile stocks' gains due to concerns about weaker price competitiveness in overseas markets.\par \par \uc2 "A weak yen is putting a pressure on information-technology-parts shares such as Samsung SDI and Cheil Industries, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  and \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  won't rise further," said Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . Samsung SDI was down 6.1%, Cheil Industries lost 7.5%.\par \par \uc2 China property developers were extending their losses. "The uncertainty over whether Beijing will launch further tightening measures for the property market continues to weigh on developers and banks," said Deng Wenyuan at Soochow Securities. He added that the market was especially cautious ahead of next week's three-day mid-autumn festival holiday, as China in the past had chosen holiday periods to launch new policies.\par \par \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  was down 0.5%, \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  was off 1.5%. \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  shed 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, Taiwan's Taiex was 0.1% lower, Singapore's Straits Times Index was up 0.3%, Malaysia's KLCI was off 0.3%, Philippine shares were down 0.2%, shares in Thailand were down 0.7%, Indonesian stocks up 2.9% and New Zealand's NZX-50 was up 0.1%.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets started the day in quiet trade but were shaken up by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar was last at Y84.94, from Y83.08 in New York, while the euro was at $1.2967, from $1.3020 and Y110.13 from Y108.20.\par \par \uc2 Hideki Amikura, deputy general manager at Nomura Trust and Banking said the yen may remain strong in the weeks ahead despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly if the U.S. Federal Reserve moves toward further easing and Treasury yields fall: "I'd give the yen about a 60% chance of staying strong."\par \par \uc2 He said while the dollar could rise to around Y85.00, substantial selling by Japanese exporters there would likely weigh on the greenback. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at least likely to have put a floor under the dollar at Y83.00, he said; "that could become support for now, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted after the breach of that level."\par \par \uc2 The greenback rebounded against the Korean won after the Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , from a low of KRW1,157.2 to KRW1,164.5, compared with KRW1,161.7 late Tuesday in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 "A weakening of the yen is a negative for emerging-market Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Dariusz Kowalczyk, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , noting that the Korean won and New Taiwan dollar were at biggest risk of weakening, given those economies' export mix, which are fairly similar to Japan's.\par \par \uc2 The dollar inched higher against the Chinese yuan despite yet another record-low fixing by the People's Bank of China, rising to CNY6.7374 after the moves in Japan, well above its intraday low at CNY6.7300.\par \par \uc2 Lead December Japanese government bond futures pared their earlier gains, hitting an intraday low of 141.74 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Nikkei's rise. The contract was last up 0.42 at 141.80 points. The 10-year cash JGB yield was down 6.5 bps at 1.065%.\par \par \uc2 October Nymex crude oil futures were down 63 cents at $76.17 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -Colin Ng, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; colin.ng@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 15-09-10 0519GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100915e69f000bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
