\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen Mixed After Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 3,319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses, government debt seen mixed; euro, sterling, dollar up vs yen post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; oil futures, spot gold lower\par \par \uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market; Cites Instability\par \par \uc2 -Global Oil Price Should Be Much Higher: IEA Chief Economist\par \par \uc2 -Vodafone Deutschland To Award Maintenance To Ericsson -Report\par \par \uc2 -France's EdF Prices EUR1 Billion 5.125% 2050 Bond At 98.058\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2 : Prosecutor Starts Bribery Probe Proceedings\par \par \uc2 -BP Cited For Safety Lapses In North Sea - FT\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: WTO Likely To Rule \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Got Illegal Subsidies From US -Sources\par \par \uc2 -EU's Barnier Sees "Revolution" For Derivatives Rules -Report\par \par \uc2 -France's Lagarde: "Crisis Not Totally Behind Us" -Report\par \par \uc2 -Axel Springer Plans To Place 2.9M Of Own Shares -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Cisco\uc2  Says Dividend Yielding 1%-2% Likely In FY11\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0830 GMT UK Aug Monthly UK unemployment figures; 0900 GMT EU Aug Harmonised CPI; 1315 GMT US Aug Industrial Production & Capacity Utilization\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have hurt investors holding long positions on the yen, but dealers doubt the action will have a lasting effect. European bourses and government bond markets, seen mixed at the opening, remain focused on economic issues. The euro and sterling are higher against the yen, while spot gold and oil futures are lower.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are likely to start narrowly mixed on Wednesday, as investors weigh Europe's slowing expansion against reassuring data of late showing growth in China and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 For Wednesday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE off four at 5563, the DAX up four at 6279 and the CAC down seven at 3767.\par \par \uc2 "The drop in (German) ZEW economic sentiment in September and July's stagnation in euro-zone industrial production confirm that the euro-zone recovery is slowing again after the second quarter's strong expansion," said Capital Economics analyst Jennifer McKeown.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., "The last three weeks have seen a rise of nearly 500 points for the FTSE 100 and it looks as if some are happy to just bank profits up here at four-month highs," said \uc2 IG Index\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are slightly higher on Wednesday, after markets fell slightly Tuesday as investors worried about the economy despite better-than-expected signs from the retail sector that boosted \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , \uc2 J.C. Penney\uc2  and \uc2 Kroger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The better-than-expected reports from the retail sector represent "one of the signs people have been waiting for," said Frances Hudson, global thematic strategist at \uc2 Standard Life Investments\uc2 . "You're seeing pockets of resilience."\par \par \uc2 Still, Hudson noted that investors remain concerned about the future of the economy.\par \par \uc2 "Quite a lot of the macro data is not supportive" of stocks, she said.\par \par \uc2 "This is slightly better than expected and shows growth and it will be supportive, but at the end of the day it is one of a myriad of numbers driving the economy," said Jason Schenker, president of Prestige Economics, of the retail data.\par \par \uc2 "This piece of data does seem to support the notion that we are not likely to have a double-dip recession so this was likely to buttress prices," he said.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks are mixed Wednesday while the Tokyo market staged a stunning turnaround after the yen fell sharply due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen is scrambling the outlook in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with the resurgent euro now called a sell against the dollar, even after some analysts had called $1.35 a target.\par \par \uc2 Local Tokyo traders estimated that authorities' yen sales were worth around Y200 billion to Y300 billion in the early minutes of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , their first in six years.\par \par \uc2 That sent the greenback surging toward the Y85 level, compared with an earlier low of Y82.87. The euro tagged along and rose past Y110 from Y107.74 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda confirmed that the government was selling the yen and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued even as the dollar climbed above Y84.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like putting a band aid on a person, in this case a country bleeding from deflation...this (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not a fundamental solution," said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts pointed to a \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group, Inc\uc2 . (GS) conference call, during which Goldman said the Fed could announce a new asset-purchase program to kick-start a moribund U.S. economy, as contributing to the dollar's weakness Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A new asset-purchase program, which Goldman said could start as early as November, would weigh further on the dollar because it would be taken as a signal that interest rates would remain ultralow for an extended period. Investors use cheap dollars to fund bets in higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 Fed action would solidify worries about a slowing U.S. economy, which could explain the bifurcated moves in markets Tuesday, with safe-harbor assets gaining alongside \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  closely tied to the pace of global growth.\par \par \uc2 Slightly better-than-expected U.S. retail sales figures released Tuesday failed to allay worries over the pace of the U.S. recovery, said Alan Ruskin, global head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "It does not detract from market concerns that another dose of (quantitative easing) still lies in the wings for later this year," Ruskin said.\par \par \uc2 And even if the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  does not raise rates Thursday, it will likely be "looking toward doing more policy tightening," said Brian Kim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 , noting his firm called for the SNB to hike key rates by 25 basis points, though the consensus of expectations calls for the SNB to stand pat on rates.\par \par \uc2 Once the impact of the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  subsides, the pound is likely to gain against the dollar while easing lower against the euro.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts are likely to start mixed on Wednesday, with more supply on tap amid important economic data that should provide more guidance for risk tolerance levels.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, signs of slowing growth in Europe offset robust demand at a Greek Treasury bill sale and a pick-up in U.S. retail sales.\par \par \uc2 Investors will especially await the reception given to a EUR5 billion bund auction and more euro-zone data on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Euro-zone August consumer price inflation is expected to have eased from July, in line with the preliminary reading reported late August.\par \par \uc2 "The flash print of 1.6% year-on-year should be confirmed, with energy base effects dragging headline inflation lower core," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  economists said in a weekly research note.\par \par \uc2 While gilts are still under the sway of developments overseas, the market shrugged off data showing stubborn core inflation in the U.K. Recent data in the U.K. have also pointed to a slowdown in activity, and any rise in yields will be capped for now, said Philip Tyson, head of rates strategy at \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . "We continue to favour a re-flattening of the gilt curve," Tyson said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Bank of England Governor Mervyn King will be giving a speech, the Debt Management Office will auction GBP2 billion of UKT 2030 securities, and U.K. labor data will also be released.\par \par \uc2 August unemployment data are likely to show a further, albeit small decline in the up-to-date claimant count reading. Economists also expect no change in the percentage of unemployed persons as calculated by an international measure in the three months to July.\par \par \uc2 "Unemployment may edge down further in the near term, but we suspect that it could well start heading back up before the end of this year and then increase further in 2011," said Howard Archer, chief U.K. and euro zone economist for IHS Global Insight.\par \par \uc2 "Major job losses are on the way in the public sector as the government slashes spending, and we doubt that the private sector will be able to fully compensate for this."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury prices are lower on Wednesday, after markets gained for a second day on Tuesday, as analysts pointed to continued demand for bonds after a three-week sell-off, shrugging off solid data on retail sales and business inventories.\par \par \uc2 With 10-year yields up from 2.47% at the end of August, Treasury prices "appear to have sold off a bit too far, a bit too fast," said George Goncalves, a bond strategist at Nomura.\par \par \uc2 This week, the Federal Reserve is buying up Treasury bonds from the market and the government isn't selling any debt, "which perhaps provides the breathing space Treasurys need to recoup some of last week's sharp losses after long-end duration hit the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo Wednesday, lead December Japanese government bond futures pared their earlier gains, hitting an intraday low of 141.74 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Nikkei's rise. The contract was last up 0.42 at 141.80 points. The 10-year cash JGB yield was down 6.5 bps at 1.065%.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude prices are lower Wednesday following news that repairs to a key pipeline carrying oil from Canada to the U.S. were almost complete, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in October, fell 51 cents to $76.29 a barrel. Brent North Sea crude for October delivery slipped five cents to $79.11.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in oil prices came after Enbridge said that repairs to its key Line 6A Canadian-U.S. pipeline were nearly complete," the \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  said in a report.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Spot gold is down $1.10 cents from late New York levels at $1,267.60 a troy ounce, after the metal ran to a record high of $1,274.75. In a monthly update, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  said it expects gold to hold current levels in the short-term. It said it'll take some time for the turbulence in financial markets to subside, so safe haven demand will persist as the possibility of a sovereign default or another economic contraction lingers. [ 15-09-10 0500GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Copper, while marginally lower Wednesday, continues to be most favored base metal, said Citi, and "we find it hard to fault its underlying fundamentals."\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 15-09-10 0500GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10526.49  -17.64  -0.17% \par Nasdaq     2289.77   +4.06  +0.18% \par S&P 500    1121.10   -0.80  -0.07% \par FTSE 100   5567.41   +1.88  +0.03% \par Xetra DAX  6275.41  +13.73  +0.22% \par CAC40      3774.40    7.25   0.19% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9543.07  243.76  2.62% \par Hang Seng    21694.08  -1.96  -0.01% \par S&P/ASX 200   4647.00  20.54   0.44% \par Taiwan Index  8138.50   5.90   0.07% \par S.Korea Kospi 1816.73   1.48   0.08% \par Shanghai Comp 2673.08  -15.45 -0.57% \par Bombay SenSex 19468.40 121.44  0.63% \par Dow Future    10470.00 +7.00   +0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1926.25  +3.75   +0.2% \par S&P Future    1116.80  +1.00   +0.1% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      84.89-93     +2.18% \par Range        84.96   82.88 \par EUR/USD      1.2956-60    -0.30% \par Range        1.3022  1.2956 \par AUD/USD      0.9357-61    -0.51% \par Range        0.9428  0.9358 \par GBP/USD      1.5486-90    -0.40% \par Range        1.5555  1.5482 \par USD/CHF      1.0022-31    +0.67% \par Range        1.0032  0.9950 \par EUR/GBP      0.8364-69    +0.11% \par Range        0.8383  0.8358 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2994-96    +0.39% \par Range        1.3012  1.2930 \par Above are as of 0350 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 12.16%/12.84% \par Day earlier: 11.46%/12.14% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 10.76%/11.38% \par Day earlier: 10.34%/10.96% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Con