\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK:Nikkei May Rise;9550-9750 Range Seen, USD/JPY Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2010\par 01:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2308 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei may rise, with exporters extending gains as government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped lift USD/JPY above 85 from 15-year low; index may trade between 9550-9750 after ending up 2.3% at 9516.56 yesterday (amid heavy volume), say market analysts. "Worries about corporate earnings are receding," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. But analysts say monetary authorities still face uphill battle as they strive to curb upward pressure on yen; rates continue to be monitored closely. Commodity stocks may underperform on falling gold and crude futures, they add. Nikkei 225 December futures ended yesterday's Chicago trading at 9605 vs Osaka close of 9470. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  had pencilled in RBNZ to hike in December, but there's significant doubt over that now, and indeed on timing of their next move given much domestic uncertainty, says economist Robin Clements. Expects price pressures to remain on inflation and good growth in trading partners to filter through to local economy. "Nonetheless, this still means a path for policy rates that will involve a mixture of hikes and pauses, with the timing sensitive to the state of the global and domestic outlook." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD likely to have a quiet session with focus on speeches by Treasury Secretary Ken Henry and RBA Assistant Governor Phil Lowe. Some chance that they may provide more directives on the outlook for monetary policy. Grant Turley, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ANZ, says elsewhere attention will be on BOJ activity. For the moment, "it is all quiet on the western front". Adds AUD cross has been performing strongly, so some profit taking may emerge ahead of the weekend. Expects resistance at 0.9420 with support at 0.9350. Now 0.9384. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's jobless rate likely held steady at 4.3% in June-August period, according to median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Still, economists say it could fall toward year-end as city's economy continues to grow. "Domestic demand remains robust due to the strength of the local economy, and the situation surrounding Europe's sovereign debt crisis is better-than-expected, which helped to support Hong Kong's labor market," says \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang. Tips jobless rate to fall to 4.0% by year-end. Data due 0830 GMT Thursday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ have swung from being too bullish in their outlook to being too bearish and now look too conservative in their outlook for 2011 growth, says ANZ economist Khoon Goh; doesn't expect a hike now before March 2011, with 4.5% peak for OCR now likely. "We believe the RBNZ was too bullish on growth three months ago and commentators talking a 6%-plus OCR endgame, but are now coming in too bearish on 2011 prospects, and hence low on their assessment of where rates will end up." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD has likely done bulk of its drop after RBNZ statement, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Bernard Doyle; while most in market consider statement dovish, Doyle says: "We believe this is effectively a neutral stance." NZD/USD dropped 40 pips after statement to low of 0.7260, last at 0.7274. "However, we doubt there will be much follow through on this. The market may be reacting to the extent of the lowering in the 90-day bill track in the RBNZ's forecasts." RBNZ slashed its 90 day bills forecast to 3.4% in 4Q 2010 and 4.1% 4Q 2011 from 3.7% and 5.3%.(enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mixed, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.2% at 4668 after Wall Street rose despite weak economic data and weaker commodity prices. DJIA up 0.4% at 10572.73, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1125.07, Nasdaq up 0.5% at 2301.32. U.S. August industrial production rose 0.2% vs 0.3% expected. Capacity utilization rose to 74.7% vs 75.0% expected. S&P 500 Consumer Staples up 0.7%, with Kraft up 1.7% on upbeat outlook statements. S&P 500 Healthcare up 0.8%, with McKesson up 5.4% after reiterating earnings guidance. S&P 500 Financials up 0.4%, with KBW Bank index up 0.1%. S&P 500 Materials down 0.1%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 0.5%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 2.2%, Barrick Gold down 0.5%. BHP ADRs at A$39.13 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9383) vs local close at A$39.44. London listed BHP down 0.9%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 1.4%. S&P 500 Industrials up 0.1%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.2%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary up 0.2%. Spot gold at US$1,267.78 vs US$1,268.53. Nymex crude oil down 1.0% at US$76.02. LME copper down 0.5%, zinc down 1.4%, aluminum down 0.3%, nickel down 0.6%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3012 vs 1.2993. AUD/USD at 0.9379 vs 0.9398. S&P/ASX 200 last 4661.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to remain focus for markets; USD/JPY largely in range in most of European, U.S. session Wednesday after spiking in Asian trade as Tokyo waded into market; Japanese government official says will extend \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed; investors will be watching their resolve in keeping the pair up or if they move to drive it even higher; USD/JPY at 85.67 vs 85.63 late Wednesday in NY, EUR/JPY at 111.44 vs 111.36, EUR/USD at 1.3011 vs 1.3012. NZD/USD pressured lower, last at 0.7271 after RBNZ keeps rates on hold as widely expected early Thursday but decidedly cools its heels on projections for pace of any future tightening, citing impact of Christchurch earthquake, uncertain outlook for domestic, world growth. Asian stock markets have slightly positive lead from Wall Street's gains, despite weaker-than-expected NY Fed manufacturing survey, U.S. industrial production; Japanese exporters could extend gains on USD/JPY strength. Malaysian markets shut for holiday. On data slate: Japan tertiary industry index at 2350 GMT, Australia consumer inflationary expectations survey at 0100 GMT, RBA bulletin at 0130 GMT, India weekly WPI at 0630 GMT, Hong Kong unemployment at 0830 GMT, RBI policy review, BOJ governor Shirakawa speaks. Later there's ECB governing council meeting, UK retail sales, CBI industrial trends, EU trade balance, U.S. PPI, jobless claims, TICS data, foreign U.S. debt holdings, Fed governor Duke speaks, Treasury Secretary Geithner testifies, Philly Fed business outlook survey, BOE MPC member Posen speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ gaining confidence upcoming GST price increases won't necessarily feed through to inflation as subdued domestic demand is bigger driver of price action right now, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  economist Bernard Doyle. Doesn't expect RBNZ to hike until 2011 when it's clear fragile domestic demand may have stabilised. "For now, we will leave our forecast for fresh hike unchanged at 1Q 2011. However, for us, like the RBNZ, this will depend on the domestic economy showing renewed momentum." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2234 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall after prices in U.S. slipped for a second consecutive session overnight in a setback from 1-month highs and as Egypt, world's top buyer, bought mostly French wheat in a tender. Traders remain on edge about tightened global supplies after a severe drought slashed output in former Soviet states and prompted Russia to ban grain exports. However, the markets lacked fresh weather threats or strong export news to spark a recovery, analysts said. Moreover, fears about tightening global supplies began to ease Tuesday, when Australia raised its forecast for wheat production and exports above traders' expectations. They had feared dryness in Western Australia could dent output. Soft red winter wheat on CBOT for December delivery sank 9.25 cents, or 1.3%, to US$7.2675/bushel. AUD remains strong, holding most recent gains, last AUD/USD last 0.9379, little different from this time yesterday. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$7.80 to settle at A$280.50/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia fell A$7.50 at A$308.50.(ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2228 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold loses 10 cents early in Asia at bid $1,268.10/oz, climbing after probing down to a low of $1,262.50, unable at this stage to make a fresh successful assault on record high of $1,275.75 posted Tuesday but continuing to hold most of that day's strong gains. Gold futures in NY pulled back yesterday as market participants sold holdings to cash in on gold's record highs and demand declined on a lack of market moving news. Gold prices have climbed since late July, lifted by worries about stumbling economic recoveries in U.S. and Europe. "Short term traders are taking some profits" in gold, said Frank Lesh, broker and analyst with FuturePath Trading in Chicago. "This doesn't deter anybody who is holding (gold) for the long term," he added. Gold futures fell earlier this year in the days after setting record highs. "Although the momentum has eased, precious metals remain well-bid," said Standard Bank analyst Walter de Wet, in a client note. LME base metals mostly fell yesterday, weighed down by weaker-than-expected U.S. data, speculation that China will step up measures to cool its property market. Metals managed to pare some of their losses on back of a rally in the euro, which rose against USD in late trading in Europe but gain wasn't enough to completely reverse fall in base metals prices. LME 3-month copper fell $35 vs Tuesday PM kerb to $7,615/metric ton, aluminum eased $7 at $2,153, zinc shed $28 at $2,145.00, nickel slipped $150 to $23,200, lead lost $7 to $2,235 but tin extended its rally, surging $505 at $23,150. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia loses 4 cents at $20.56/oz, platinum down $4 at $1,602, palladium unchanged at $554. Nymex October crude futures lose 78 cents overnight at $75.82/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 15 Sep 2010 19:08 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK:Nikkei May Rise;9550-9750 Range Seen, -2-\par \par \uc2 2218 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD/JPY surges Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for 1st time in 6 years; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  says would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Thursday if necessary. USD/JPY at 85.63 late Wednesday in NY, vs 83.08 late Tuesday; EUR/JPY at 111.36 vs 108.20, EUR/USD at 1.3012 vs 1.3020. U.S. stocks up Wednesday on encouraging forecasts from \uc2 Kraft Foods\uc2  and McKesson boosted outlook for corporate sector; DJIA +0.4%, S&P 500 +0.4%, Nasdaq +0.5%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts demand for short-dated Treasurys while weakening long-dated securities; 10-year, 30-year maturities bore brunt of sales in choppy trade on belief short-dated notes among Japan's favored places to park its USD proceeds. 10-year note price down 11/32, yield up to 2.721%; 30-year bond down 1 7/32 to yield 3.869% while 3-year note up 1/32 to yield 0.766%, 2-year note up 1/32 to yield 0.488%. Oil futures prices lower on stronger USD/JPY, also as NY Fed manufacturing survey drops to lowest level since July 2009; Empire State Manufacturing index at 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August, well below 7.0 expected. October crude down 78 cents at $76.02/bbl. LME base metals lower on U.S. data (including weaker-than-expected industrial production at +0.2% in August), concerns of possibility of Chinese measures to cool property market; copper bid down $35 at $7615/ton, lead down $7 at $2235, zinc down $28 at $2145, aluminum down $7 at $2153. Gold futures pulled back as participants cashed in on its record highs recently; December Comex gold down $3 to $1,268.70/oz. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 19:08 ET (23:08 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f005zw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
